<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno" lang="fr">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Tautological classes with twisted coefficients</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author" lang="en">
<span class="ltx_personname" lang="fr">Dan Petersen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" lang="fr">dan.petersen@math.su.se</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">, </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author" lang="en">
<span class="ltx_personname" lang="fr">Mehdi Tavakol
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text" lang="fr">mehdi.tavakol@unimelb.edu.au</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author" lang="en">
<span class="ltx_personname" lang="fr">Qizheng Yin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text" lang="fr">qizheng@math.pku.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract" lang="en"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli space of smooth genus <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves. We define a notion of Chow groups of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with coefficients in a representation of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we define a subgroup of tautological classes in these Chow groups with twisted coefficients. Studying the tautological groups of <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with twisted coefficients is equivalent to studying the tautological rings of all fibered powers <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of the universal curve <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously. By taking the direct sum over all irreducible representations of the symplectic group in fixed genus, one obtains the structure of a twisted commutative algebra on the tautological classes. We obtain some structural results for this twisted commutative algebra, and we are able to calculate it explicitly when <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we completely determine the tautological rings of all fibered powers of the universal curve over <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in these genera. We also give some applications to the Faber conjecture.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords" lang="en">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6><span id="id4" class="ltx_text" lang="fr">tautological rings, moduli spaces of curves, Chow motives, twisted commutative algebras</span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification" lang="en"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2010 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6><span id="id5" class="ltx_text" lang="fr">14H10, 14C25, 14C17, 18D10, 55R35</span>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Say that <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the moduli space of smooth genus <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves with <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> ordered not necessarily distinct marked points. Equivalently, <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fibered power of the universal curve over <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with itself. Suppose that we want to study the cohomology of the spaces <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. A natural approach is to apply the Leray–Serre spectral sequence for the fibration <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that forgets the <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> markings. Since <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper, the spectral sequence degenerates by Deligne’s decomposition theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del68</a>]</cite>, and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(M_{g},R^{q}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(M_{g},R^{q}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To each dominant weight <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> there is associated a local system <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the moduli space of principally polarized abelian varieties of dimension <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sheaves <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{q}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{q}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> decompose into direct sums of local systems <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we use the notation <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> also for their pullback along the Torelli map. The local systems <math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> occuring as summands of <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely those with <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It follows that the two collections of cohomology groups</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.Ex2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Ex2.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq N</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S1.Ex2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">    </span><span id="S1.Ex2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">and</span><span id="S1.Ex2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">    </span><span id="S1.Ex2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Ex2.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> </span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">contain more or less the same information. However, this information is “packaged” in a much more efficient way in the local systems. The cohomology groups of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are generally very large, but when expressed in terms of local systems we see that most of the cohomology just encodes how the complex <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into summands — that is, it encodes the Künneth formula for the <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold self-product of a genus <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curve, and some representation theory of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>. By studying the local systems we may focus our attention on the “interesting” part of the cohomology in a systematic way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our first goal of this paper is to do the same thing for the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological rings</em> of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We remind the reader that the tautological ring <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the subalgebra of <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by the classes of the diagonal loci <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> where two markings coincide, the classes <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which are the Chern classes of the <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> cotangent line bundles at the marked points, and the Morita–Mumford–Miller classes <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The image of <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in cohomology under the cycle class map is denoted <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will be able to define tautological cohomology groups <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\subseteq H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\subseteq H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>, with the property that the collections of tautological groups</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.Ex3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Ex3.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq N</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S1.Ex3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">    </span><span id="S1.Ex3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">and</span><span id="S1.Ex3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">    </span><span id="S1.Ex3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Ex3.m1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> </span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">bear exactly the same relation to each other as the collections of cohomology groups <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we are able to decompose the tautological groups of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into pieces indexed by local systems; the tautological groups of the local systems package all the information about the tautological groups of <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in a much more efficient way, and working with twisted coefficients allows us to “zoom in” on particularly interesting parts of the tautological groups. Moreover, the groups <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> turn out to be more computable than the groups <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, we will actually not only do this on the level of cohomology groups, but for Chow groups. (The results are new already on the level of cohomology, though.) For this we should not work with local systems on <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, but with relative Chow motives over the base <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead of decomposing the complex <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> into local systems <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will decompose the Chow motive <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> into Chow motives <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> which are motivic lifts of the local systems <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once the correct framework is in place, working with motives rather than local systems provides no extra difficulties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The utility of working with the local systems is illustrated by our Theorem <a href="#S10.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 10.1. ‣ 10. Low genus calculations ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>, in which we completely determine all tautological groups with twisted coefficients when <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math>. It is an easy matter to compute from Theorem <a href="#S10.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 10.1. ‣ 10. Low genus calculations ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a> the ranks of all the groups <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the decompositions of these tautological groups into <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representations, and the socle pairing. Thus a lot of useful information about the tautological rings is encoded in a few lines of information about the local systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since the tautological rings are defined in terms of explicit generators, understanding the tautological rings is equivalent to finding the complete list of relations between these generators. A conjectural complete description of the tautological rings was formulated by Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fab99b</a>]</cite>. Namely, a theorem of Looijenga <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo95</a>]</cite> asserts that <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the tautological ring vanishes above this degree. Thus any two monomials of degree <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math> in the generators of the tautological ring are proportial to each other, and the proof of the <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GP98</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giv01</a>]</cite> gives explicit proportionalities. (In fact, both Looijenga’s theorem and the proportionalities were part of Faber’s original conjecture.) What Faber then conjectured was that any possible relation which is consistent with the pairing into the top degree is a true relation; that is, the ring <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> should satisfy Poincaré duality. The general belief is now that this conjecture should fail. One reason is that the original conjecture was later extended to a “trinity” of conjectures for the spaces <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{ct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝖼𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{ct}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fab01</a>]</cite>, and the conjectures for <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{2,n}^{\mathsf{ct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝖼𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{2,n}^{\mathsf{ct}}</annotation></semantics></math> are known to fail when <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx63" title="" class="ltx_ref">PT14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pet16b</a>]</cite>. The Faber conjecture for the spaces <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the Faber conjecture for <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pet16a</a>]</cite>, and is still open. It has more recently been conjectured that Pixton’s extension of the FZ relations (see Section <a href="#S9" title="9. A consequence of the FZ relations ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) give rise to all relations between tautological classes, and this conjecture is known to contradict the Faber conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pix13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">An interesting aspect of our work is that even though the decomposition of the tautological groups <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> into pieces indexed by representations of <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> is not compatible with the ring structure, the multiplication into the top degree behaves very well: the matrix describing the top degree pairing is block diagonal with respect to our decomposition of the tautological groups. This has the consequence that the Faber conjecture can be fruitfully studied from the perspective of the motives <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> — Poincaré duality can be checked for each <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> separately. Using this we show that the Faber conjecture is true for the moduli space <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (hence also the space <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) when <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary, and we make some progress in trying to understand likely failures of the Faber conjectures in higher genera.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">A completely different perspective on our results is provided by work of Kawazumi–Morita and Hain. For a fixed genus <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, one can define a structure of commutative ring on the direct sum</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}_{g}=\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}_{g}=\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where the direct sum is taken over all dominant weights <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}=\bigwedge\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}/(\mathbb{V}_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>⋀</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}=\bigwedge\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}/(\mathbb{V}_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the exterior algebra on the representation <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, modulo the ideal generated by the subrepresentation <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\wedge^{2}\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>𝕍</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\wedge^{2}\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is placed in degree <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, then one can define a natural <math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant homomorphism of graded commutative rings <math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we get a morphism between the subalgebras of symplectic invariants,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}=H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}=H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">According to a theorem of Kawazumi–Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM96</a>]</cite> the image of <math id="S1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the tautological cohomology ring of <math id="S1.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this reason it is natural, following Hain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai13</a>]</cite>, to define the image of <math id="S1.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> as the “tautological subalgebra” <math id="S1.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}\subset\mathsf{T}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}\subset\mathsf{T}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. By considering the individual summands of <math id="S1.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> one obtains an a priori completely different definition of tautological subgroup of <math id="S1.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove in Theorem <a href="#S12.Thmthm8" title="Theorem 12.8. ‣ 12. The “primary approximation” to the cohomology of the moduli space ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.8</span></a> that the two definitions coincide, which in particular re-proves the theorem of Kawazumi–Morita.
Our low genus results can be seen as calculations of the ring <math id="S1.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>; these are the first nontrivial cases where this ring is completely known. A consequence of our general results is that the morphism <math id="S1.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> can be lifted to take values in Chow groups rather than cohomology groups, answering a question of Hain. A final remark is that Morita has conjectured <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor06</a>]</cite> that the morphism <math id="S1.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, so that <math id="S1.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">isomorphic</em> to the tautological ring of <math id="S1.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. By extension it is natural to ask also whether <math id="S1.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. Our results in genus four show that this is not the case, however: <math id="S1.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{4}\to\mathsf{R}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{4}\to\mathsf{R}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is not an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now state in some more detail what we do in this paper:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any partition <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\ldots\geq\lambda_{g}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\lambda_{2}\geq\ldots\geq\lambda_{g}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a relative Chow motive <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> over the moduli space <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a motivic version of the local system over <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a representation of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> of highest weight <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> be the relative Chow motive over <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fibered power of the universal curve. We prove that there exists a direct sum decomposition</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Lefschetz motive, and in particular we get upon taking Chow groups</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathrm{CH}^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathrm{CH}^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct an algebra of correspondences defined by tautological classes, which acts on the motives <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence on the Chow groups <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this algebra we obtain a <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">canonical</em> choice of decomposition (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In item ii ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and a method for computing the projection of any class in <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> into any particular summand on the right hand side of (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In item ii ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define subgroups <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\subseteq\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\subseteq\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that for any decomposition of <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> as in Eq. (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In item ii ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\bigoplus_{i}R^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\bigoplus_{i}R^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We call the groups <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological groups of <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with twisted coefficients.</em> Informally, all information about the tautological rings <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the groups <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(v)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The motives <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> come with a duality pairing <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\to\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\to\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the motivic avatar of the fact that all representations of the symplectic group are self-dual. We prove that the socle pairing</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})\to R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})\to R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a direct sum of pairings of the form</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2+|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|})=R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2+|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|})=R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Gorenstein algebra — that is, (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In item v ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is a perfect pairing for all <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> — if and only if (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In item v ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is a perfect pairing for all <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S1.I1.i5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(vi)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we fix a genus and consider the <em id="S1.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">direct sum</em> over all partitions,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{\lambda}R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\lambda}R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the representation of the symmetric group corresponding to the partition conjugate to <math id="S1.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the structure of a <em id="S1.I1.i6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted commutative algebra</em>. The <math id="S1.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> component of this twisted commutative algebra is just the tautological ring of <math id="S1.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove using the FZ relations that this twisted commutative algebra is finitely generated with an explicit bound for the degrees of the generators, which for <math id="S1.I1.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> specializes to the theorem of Ionel–Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ion05</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor03</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(vii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math> we completely determine the groups <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. A key input is that the twisted commutative algebra described in point (vi) above will in these low genera have only <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> generators, respectively, by our generalization of the theorem of Ionel–Morita. As a consequence we can compute the ranks <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in these genera, and how these tautological groups decompose into irreducible representations of <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It also follows that <math id="S1.I1.i7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is always a Gorenstein algebra in these genera.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The algebra of projectors described in point (iii) above seems like a particularly powerful tool for “zooming in” on a specific part of the tautological ring. As explained in Section <a href="#S10.SS4" title="10.4. Genus five ‣ 10. Low genus calculations ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.4</span></a> we expect that the Faber conjecture fails when <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> for the motives <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2,2,2,2\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2,2,2,2\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle 3,2,2,1\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle 3,2,2,1\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using our algebra of projectors we can project specific tautological classes onto these summands, which gives explicit classes which pair to zero with everything in complementary degree but which are conjecturally nonzero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It would be interesting to try to extend our results from <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to the Deligne–Mumford compactification <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the level of cohomology this would correspond to studying the forgetful maps <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon\overline{{M}}_{g,n}\to\overline{{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon\overline{{M}}_{g,n}\to\overline{{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer smooth, but still proper, so by the decomposition theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">BBD82</a>]</cite> the complex <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of perverse sheaves. In fact, each of these perverse sheaves will be the pushforward along some gluing map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Big(\!\prod_{v\in\mathrm{Vert}(\Gamma)}\!\!\!\overline{{M}}_{g(v),n(v)}\Big)/\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)\to\overline{{M}}_{g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Vert</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(\!\prod_{v\in\mathrm{Vert}(\Gamma)}\!\!\!\overline{{M}}_{g(v),n(v)}\Big)/\mathrm{Aut}(\Gamma)\to\overline{{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">of the intermediate extension of a product of local systems associated to representations of the smaller symplectic groups <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g(v))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g(v))</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests that one should try to define a subspace of tautological classes inside the intersection cohomology groups <math id="S1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{I\!H}^{\bullet}(\overline{{M}}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.348em;" width="0.348em"><mi>I</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{I\!H}^{\bullet}(\overline{{M}}_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for each genus <math id="S1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and dominant weight <math id="S1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and that these tautological groups should “govern” all of the tautological groups <math id="S1.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(\overline{{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(\overline{{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> much in the same way as the tautological groups of <math id="S1.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> govern all the tautological groups <math id="S1.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly there should be tautological classes inside <math id="S1.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{I\!H}^{\bullet}(M_{g}^{\mathsf{ct}},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})=H^{\bullet}(M_{g}^{\mathsf{ct}},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.348em;" width="0.348em"><mi>I</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝖼𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>𝖼𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{I\!H}^{\bullet}(M_{g}^{\mathsf{ct}},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})=H^{\bullet}(M_{g}^{\mathsf{ct}},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> which govern all the tautological rings <math id="S1.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{ct}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝖼𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{ct}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pet16b</a>, Theorem 3.4]</cite> can be seen as calculating the tautological subspace of <math id="S1.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{2}^{\mathsf{ct}},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝖼𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{2}^{\mathsf{ct}},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it should be possible to carry out the suggestions in this paragraph also on the level of Chow groups, using the intersection Chow motives of Corti–Hanamura <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CH00</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>How to read this paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned already, this paper is written in the language of Chow motives. Readers who would prefer not to know what a motive is should still be able to follow the arguments by translating the arguments to cohomology using the following table:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex9.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Chow motive <math id="S1.Ex9.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> of a family <math id="S1.Ex9.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Complex <math id="S1.Ex9.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> in the derived category of <math id="S1.Ex9.m2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Decomposition <math id="S1.Ex10.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)\cong\bigoplus_{i}h^{i}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)\cong\bigoplus_{i}h^{i}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex10.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Decomposition <math id="S1.Ex10.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong\bigoplus_{i}R^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong\bigoplus_{i}R^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-i]</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex11.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Chow group <math id="S1.Ex11.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,h^{i}(X/S))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,h^{i}(X/S))</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex11.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Cohomology group <math id="S1.Ex11.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2k-i}(S,R^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2k-i}(S,R^{i}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex12.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Lefschetz motive <math id="S1.Ex12.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex12.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S1.Ex12.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="S1.Ex12.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Constant sheaf <math id="S1.Ex12.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, considered as a complex</span>
<span id="S1.Ex12.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">concentrated in degree <math id="S1.Ex12.m2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex13.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">Chow motive <math id="S1.Ex13.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.580em" minsize="1.580em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo maxsize="1.580em" minsize="1.580em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.Ex13.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S1.Ex13.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S1.Ex13.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="S1.Ex13.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Local system <math id="S1.Ex13.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.580em" minsize="1.580em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo maxsize="1.580em" minsize="1.580em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Ex13.m2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, considered as a complex</span>
<span id="S1.Ex13.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_p">concentrated in degree <math id="S1.Ex13.m2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus the only real complication is the indexing: the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th Chow group of the motive <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k-|\lambda|-2i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k-|\lambda|-2i)</annotation></semantics></math>th cohomology group of the local system <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Sections 2–4 of this paper explain necessary preliminary material from the representation theory of the symplectic group and about Chow motives. In particular, Section 4 explains a result of Ancona which is used to lift our methods from cohomology to Chow groups. It could be a good idea for the reader to start from Section <a href="#S5" title="5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and refer back to the previous sections only as needed. Section <a href="#S5" title="5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> provides the theoretical backbone to the article, and Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> provides some simple (hopefully instructive) example calculations. In Sections <a href="#S7" title="7. Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>–<a href="#S10" title="10. Low genus calculations ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> the theory is applied and our main results are proven. The concluding Sections <a href="#S11" title="11. Relation to work of Looijenga ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and <a href="#S12" title="12. The “primary approximation” to the cohomology of the moduli space ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> explain the relationship between what we do and previous work of Looijenga, Hain, Morita, and Kawazumi.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Conventions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Representations of groups will be considered as left representations unless specified otherwise. However, if <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a left representation, then we consider its dual <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> as a right representation. (Recall that the dual of a left module over a noncommutative ring is a right module, and vice versa.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Chow groups are always taken with rational coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We occasionally consider cohomology groups as well as Chow groups. Although we write cohomology with rational coefficients throughout, it will be clear that all results could have been carried out equally well in the étale setting, with coefficients in <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first author would like to thank Dick Hain and Allen Knutsen for useful comments. The second author was supported by the research grant IBS-R003-S1. We are grateful to Rahul Pandharipande for his interest in this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Chow motives</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of this paper will be formulated in the language of Chow motives. The first parts of this section briefly recall standard definitions for the reader’s convenience and to fix conventions. For a more detailed and motivated introduction see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch94</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>The category of Chow motives</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth connected scheme or Deligne–Mumford stack over a field <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that we assume algebraically closed for simplicity. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be smooth proper schemes over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
            If <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a Deligne–Mumford stack we do not impose the condition that <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are schemes, only that the maps to <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are representable in schemes.</span></span></span> We define a graded vector space of <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">correspondences over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></em> as follows: if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is of relative dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)=\mathrm{CH}^{d+\bullet}(X\times_{S}Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)=\mathrm{CH}^{d+\bullet}(X\times_{S}Y)</annotation></semantics></math>; in the general case we define <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(\coprod_{\alpha}X_{\alpha},Y)=\prod_{\alpha}\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X_{\alpha},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∐</mo><mi>α</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(\coprod_{\alpha}X_{\alpha},Y)=\prod_{\alpha}\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X_{\alpha},Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. We write</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\vdash Y" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\vdash Y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">to denote that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a correspondence from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The composition of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X\vdash Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X\vdash Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\colon Y\vdash Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\colon Y\vdash Z</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ f=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p_{12}^{\ast}(f)\cdot p_{23}^{\ast}(g))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>12</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>23</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ f=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p_{12}^{\ast}(f)\cdot p_{23}^{\ast}(g)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">(note the reversed ordering!) where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the projection from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X\times_{S}Y\times_{S}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\times_{S}Y\times_{S}Z</annotation></semantics></math> onto the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th factor of the fibered product. One checks that composition of correspondences is associative and that the diagonal, considered as a correspondence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\vdash X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\vdash X</annotation></semantics></math>, acts as the identity <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\id_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\id_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a correspondence <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon X\vdash X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon X\vdash X</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">idempotent</em> if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\circ p=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\circ p=p</annotation></semantics></math>. We also say that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">projector</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define the category <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> of <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chow motives over <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</em> The objects of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> are triples <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,p,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,p,n)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and proper over <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon X\vdash X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon X\vdash X</annotation></semantics></math> is a projector, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Morphisms are defined by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}((X,p,n),(Y,q,m))=q\circ\mathsf{Corr}_{S}^{m-n}(X,Y)\circ p\subset\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}((X,p,n),(Y,q,m))=q\circ\mathsf{Corr}_{S}^{m-n}(X,Y)\circ p\subset\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}^{r}(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}^{r}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> part of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\circ\mathsf{Corr}_{S}^{m-n}(X,Y)\circ p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\circ\mathsf{Corr}_{S}^{m-n}(X,Y)\circ p</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the joint image of the projectors <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> on the right and on the left, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lefschetz motive over <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math></em> is defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\id,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>id</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\id,-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and will be denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is clear from context we will omit the subscript and write <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We define a tensor product on motives as follows. If <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(X,p,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(X,p,n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(Y,q,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(Y,q,m)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\otimes N=(X\times_{S}Y,p\times q,n+m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\otimes N=(X\times_{S}Y,p\times q,n+m)</annotation></semantics></math>. This makes <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> a symmetric monoidal category with monoidal unit <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}=(S,\id,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}=(S,\id,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The category is in fact rigid symmetric monoidal, i.e. every object has a dual: if <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is of pure dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then the dual of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(X,p,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(X,p,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\ast}=(X,p^{t},d-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\ast}=(X,p^{t},d-n)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose correspondence. The category also has direct sums. The sum <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,p,n)\oplus(Y,q,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,p,n)\oplus(Y,q,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is easiest to define when <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=m</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case it is given by <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X\sqcup Y,p\oplus q,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X\sqcup Y,p\oplus q,n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category of smooth proper schemes over <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a contravariant functor <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{S}\to\mathsf{Mot}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{S}\to\mathsf{Mot}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> which is given on objects by <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto(X,\id,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>id</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto(X,\id,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and which maps an <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>-morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> to the class of the transpose of its graph in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\dim_{S}(X)}(X\times_{S}Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\dim_{S}(X)}(X\times_{S}Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> smooth and proper we denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding Chow motive over <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h(X/S)^{\ast}\cong h(X/S)\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-\dim_{S}X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)^{\ast}\cong h(X/S)\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-\dim_{S}X}</annotation></semantics></math> (Poincaré duality).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Chow groups and cohomology groups of a Chow motive</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a Chow motive over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. We define its Chow groups by
<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)=\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbb{L}_{S}^{k},M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)=\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbb{L}_{S}^{k},M)</annotation></semantics></math>. We can make this definition more explicit as follows. Note that for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth proper scheme over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(X)=\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(S,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(X)=\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(S,X)</annotation></semantics></math>. As such, the algebra <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,X)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the Chow groups of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on the left. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=(X,p,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=(X,p,n)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Chow motive over <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then its Chow groups are given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)=p\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k+n}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)=p\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k+n}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is also true that <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(X)=\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(X)=\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,S)</annotation></semantics></math> (up to a degree shift), so that <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,X)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the Chow groups of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on the right. One could also define <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)=\mathrm{CH}^{k+n}(X)\circ p^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)=\mathrm{CH}^{k+n}(X)\circ p^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose correspondence of <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us suppose that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex algebraic variety. There is a <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Betti realization</em> functor <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{real}:\mathsf{Mot}_{S}\to D^{b}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{real}:\mathsf{Mot}_{S}\to D^{b}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> into the bounded derived category of sheaves of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> a smooth proper scheme over <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{real}\,h(X/S)=R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{real}\,h(X/S)=R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The algebra <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗋𝗋</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Corr}_{S}(X,X)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the complex <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon X\vdash X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon X\vdash X</annotation></semantics></math> idempotent we define <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{real}\,(X,p,n)=\mathrm{Im}(p_{\ast}\colon R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\to R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})[2n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{real}\,(X,p,n)=\mathrm{Im}(p_{\ast}\colon R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\to R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})[2n]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[2n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[2n]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the suspension functor in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{b}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{b}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a <em id="S2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cycle class map</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)\to\mathbb{H}^{2k}(S,\mathsf{real}\,M)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,M)\to\mathbb{H}^{2k}(S,\mathsf{real}\,M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">(where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes hypercohomology) which on motives of the form <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the usual cycle class map:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(X)=\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,h(X/S))\to\mathbb{H}^{2k}(S,\mathsf{real}\,M)=\mathbb{H}^{2k}(S,R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})=H^{2k}(X,\mathbf{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(X)=\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,h(X/S))\to\mathbb{H}^{2k}(S,\mathsf{real}\,M)=\mathbb{H}^{2k}(S,R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})=H^{2k}(X,\mathbf{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Over an arbitrary field there is an analogous realization functor from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to the derived category of étale <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>-sheaves on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Künneth decomposition, the summands <math id="S2.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2d}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be smooth, proper and purely of relative dimension <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. By Deligne’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del68</a>]</cite> there is an isomorphism in <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{b}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{b}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2d}R^{i}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-i]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2d}R^{i}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-i].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, this decomposition implies that the Leray spectral sequence for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(X,\mathbf{Q})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(S,R^{q}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(X,\mathbf{Q})\cong\bigoplus_{p+q=k}H^{p}(S,R^{q}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math>
It is expected that this decomposition always lifts to the category of Chow motives. Thus there should be an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)\cong\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2d}h^{i}(X/S)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)\cong\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2d}h^{i}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for which <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{real}\,h^{i}(X/S)\cong R^{i}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{real}\,h^{i}(X/S)\cong R^{i}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-i]</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular one would have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(X)\cong\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2d}\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,h^{i}(X/S))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(X)\cong\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2d}\mathrm{CH}^{k}(S,h^{i}(X/S)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The summands <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> can easily be constructed unconditionally. Let us suppose that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, and let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}\in\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}\in\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a cycle of degree <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on each fiber of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to S</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g. a section. One checks that the two correspondences <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\vdash X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\vdash X</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=[\mathfrak{z}\times X]\qquad\text{and}\qquad\pi_{2d}=[X\times\mathfrak{z}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔷</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=[\mathfrak{z}\times X]\qquad\text{and}\qquad\pi_{2d}=[X\times\mathfrak{z}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">are idempotent. If we define <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(X/S)=(X,\pi_{0},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(X/S)=(X,\pi_{0},0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{&gt;0}(X/S)=(X,\id_{X}-\pi_{0},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{&gt;0}(X/S)=(X,\id_{X}-\pi_{0},0)</annotation></semantics></math> then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)\cong h^{0}(X/S)\oplus h^{&gt;0}(X/S)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)\cong h^{0}(X/S)\oplus h^{&gt;0}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.6" class="ltx_p">which on realizations gives the decomposition</p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong R^{0}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\oplus\tau_{\geq 1}R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong R^{0}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\oplus\tau_{\geq 1}R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.7" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a truncation functor in the derived category <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{b}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{b}(S)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly we get decompositions <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)\cong h^{&lt;2d}(X/S)\oplus h^{2d}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)\cong h^{&lt;2d}(X/S)\oplus h^{2d}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> with realization <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\leq 2d-1}R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\oplus R^{2d}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-2d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\leq 2d-1}R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\oplus R^{2d}\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}[-2d]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}</annotation></semantics></math> be as above. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(X/S)\cong\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(X/S)\cong\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2d}(X/S)\cong\mathbb{L}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2d}(X/S)\cong\mathbb{L}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove only the second isomorphism. By definition we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbb{L}^{d},h^{2d}(X/S))=\pi_{2d}\circ\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbb{L}^{d},h^{2d}(X/S))=\pi_{2d}\circ\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(h^{2d}(X/S),\mathbb{L}^{d})=\mathrm{CH}^{0}(X)\circ\pi_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(h^{2d}(X/S),\mathbb{L}^{d})=\mathrm{CH}^{0}(X)\circ\pi_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2d}\circ\mathfrak{z}=\mathfrak{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝔷</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔷</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2d}\circ\mathfrak{z}=\mathfrak{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\circ\pi_{2d}=\pi_{0}\circ 1=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\circ\pi_{2d}=\pi_{0}\circ 1=1</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Remark <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Remark 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Chow groups and cohomology groups of a Chow motive ‣ 2. Chow motives ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). As such the cycle <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and the fundamental class <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> define morphisms <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}^{d}\to h^{2d}(X/S)\to\mathbb{L}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}^{d}\to h^{2d}(X/S)\to\mathbb{L}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, their composition in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbb{L}^{d},\mathbb{L}^{d})=\mathrm{CH}^{0}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbb{L}^{d},\mathbb{L}^{d})=\mathrm{CH}^{0}(S)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\ast}(\mathfrak{z})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔷</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\ast}(\mathfrak{z})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity. Their composition in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(h^{2d}(X/S),h^{2d}(X/S))=\pi_{2d}\circ\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X\times_{S}X)\circ\pi_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(h^{2d}(X/S),h^{2d}(X/S))=\pi_{2d}\circ\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X\times_{S}X)\circ\pi_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the correspondence <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also the identity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One might want to define a motive <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\star}(X/S)=(X,\id-\pi_{0}-\pi_{2d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>id</mi><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\star}(X/S)=(X,\id-\pi_{0}-\pi_{2d})</annotation></semantics></math> to get a decomposition <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)\cong h^{0}(X/S)\oplus h^{\star}(X/S)\oplus h^{2d}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)\cong h^{0}(X/S)\oplus h^{\star}(X/S)\oplus h^{2d}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the Betti realization of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\star}(X/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\star}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math> is the complex <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\geq 1}\tau_{\leq 2d-1}R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\geq 1}\tau_{\leq 2d-1}R\lambda_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Unfortunately the correspondence <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\id-\pi_{0}-\pi_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>id</mi><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\id-\pi_{0}-\pi_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> is not in general idempotent, since <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> are not in general orthogonal when the base scheme <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial; this will be clear from the proof of the following lemma. To make the projectors orthogonal one needs to slightly modify the cycle <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be as above. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}\in\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}\in\mathrm{CH}^{d}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be a cycle of degree <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on each fiber over <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}=\mathfrak{z}-\frac{1}{2}\lambda^{\ast}\lambda_{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}=\mathfrak{z}-\frac{1}{2}\lambda^{\ast}\lambda_{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> also has degree <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on each fiber, the projectors <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}=[\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}\times X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}=[\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}\times X]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2d}=[X\times\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2d}=[X\times\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal, and there is a decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X/S)\cong h^{0}(X/S)\oplus h^{\star}(X/S)\oplus h^{2d}(X/S)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X/S)\cong h^{0}(X/S)\oplus h^{\star}(X/S)\oplus h^{2d}(X/S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(X/S)=(X,\pi_{0},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(X/S)=(X,\pi_{0},0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\star}(X/S)=(X,\id_{X}-\pi_{0}-\pi_{2d},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\star}(X/S)=(X,\id_{X}-\pi_{0}-\pi_{2d},0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2d}(X/S)=(X,\pi_{2d},0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2d}(X/S)=(X,\pi_{2d},0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We check that <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2d}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal. We have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}\circ\pi_{2d}=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p^{\ast}_{12}(\pi_{2d})\cdot p_{23}^{\ast}(\pi_{0}))=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p_{1}^{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{\prime})\cdot p_{3}^{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}))=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>12</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>23</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}\circ\pi_{2d}=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p^{\ast}_{12}(\pi_{2d})\cdot p_{23}^{\ast}(\pi_{0}))=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p_{1}^{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{\prime})\cdot p_{3}^{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}))=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{2d}\circ\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{2d}\circ\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p^{\ast}_{12}(\pi_{0})\cdot p_{23}^{\ast}(\pi_{2d}))=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p_{2}^{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{\prime})^{2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>12</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>23</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p^{\ast}_{12}(\pi_{0})\cdot p_{23}^{\ast}(\pi_{2d}))=(p_{13})_{\ast}(p_{2}^{\ast}(\mathfrak{z}^{\prime})^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">From the cartesian diagram</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

