<p id="S3.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This composition defines in particular the structure of an associative algebra on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. This algebra is classically called the <em id="S3.SS3.p2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Brauer algebra</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be an object of a symmetric monoidal category. A <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual</em> of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is an object <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with unit and counit maps <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\ast}\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\ast}\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that both the following compositions are identities:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\cong\mathbf{1}\otimes V\to V\otimes V^{\ast}\otimes V\to V\otimes\mathbf{1}\cong V" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\cong\mathbf{1}\otimes V\to V\otimes V^{\ast}\otimes V\to V\otimes\mathbf{1}\cong V</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\ast}\cong V^{\ast}\otimes\mathbf{1}\to V^{\ast}\otimes V\otimes V^{\ast}\to\mathbf{1}\otimes V^{\ast}\cong V^{\ast}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\ast}\cong V^{\ast}\otimes\mathbf{1}\to V^{\ast}\otimes V\otimes V^{\ast}\to\mathbf{1}\otimes V^{\ast}\cong V^{\ast}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">An object <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-dual</em> if it is equipped with a pair of maps <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> making <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into its own dual. It is <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">symmetrically self-dual</em> if, in addition, the unit and counit are invariant under the flip map <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes V\to V\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes V\to V\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is dualizable, then we define the <em id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">quantum dimension</em> of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to be the element of <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End(\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End(\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math> given by the composition</p>
<table id="S3.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V^{\ast}\cong V^{\ast}\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V^{\ast}\cong V^{\ast}\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition can be seen as part of the diagrammatic calculus of “string diagrams”, describing morphisms in tensor categories (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kas95</a>, Chapter XIV]</cite>). In this calculus the precise form of the diagrams depend on the properties of the tensor category. For example, in a symmetric monoidal category strings are allowed to cross each other freely, but in a braided monoidal category the strings must be considered as braids. If we did not insist that <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> was <em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">symmetrically</em> self-dual in the following proposition, we would need to equip the strands in the Brauer algebra with orientations or framings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a symmetrically self-dual object of quantum dimension <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in a <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear symmetric monoidal category <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a natural map <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,m)\to\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(V^{\otimes n},V^{\otimes m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,m)\to\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(V^{\otimes n},V^{\otimes m})</annotation></semantics></math> which
makes the following diagram commute:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
