</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 275.4 0 C 275.4 0.76 274.78 1.38 274.02 1.38 C 273.26 1.38 272.64 0.76 272.64 0 C 272.64 -0.76 273.26 -1.38 274.02 -1.38 C 274.78 -1.38 275.4 -0.76 275.4 0 Z M 274.02 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(0.2 0.0 0.0 0.2 272.64 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="13.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.Ex48.m1.pic1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:1em;">
<span id="S3.Ex48.m1.pic1.8.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 23.62 1.49 C 23.62 17.2 0 18.23 0 33.94"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 1.49 C 0 17.2 23.62 18.23 23.62 33.94"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 274.02 1.49 C 274.02 13.46 243.31 13.46 243.31 1.49"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 118.11 33.94 C 118.11 21.97 148.82 21.97 148.82 33.94"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which generate the Brauer algebras in an appropriate sense; these diagrams are mapped to the flip map <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes V\to V\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes V\to V\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math>, the counit <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes V\to\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the unit <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\to V\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition can be formulated using the language of PROPs: the collection <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,m)\}_{n,m\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,m)\}_{n,m\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a PROP, and <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebra over this PROP in the category <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sV</annotation></semantics></math> denote the symplectic vector space <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> considered as a <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math>-graded vector space concentrated in <em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">odd</em> degree. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the monoidal unit in this category, i.e. the vector space <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> placed in even degree. The symplectic form defines “contraction” and “insertion” maps</p>
<table id="S3.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sV\otimes sV\to\mathbf{1}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathbf{1}\to sV\otimes sV." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sV\otimes sV\to\mathbf{1}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathbf{1}\to sV\otimes sV.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Taking into account the Koszul sign rule for <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math>-graded vector spaces, both these maps are now <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant; that is, by shifting <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into odd degree, we have converted the symplectic form to a symmetric bilinear form. Equivalently, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sV</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetrically self-dual in the category of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math>-graded vector spaces. The quantum dimension of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sV</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic vector space of dimension <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>. The map which sends an <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagram to a morphism <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sV)^{\otimes n}\to(sV)^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sV)^{\otimes n}\to(sV)^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the following diagram commute:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex50.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="69" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-34.5px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 609.97 69" width="609.97"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,69) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(304.99,0) translate(0,34.5)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -309.14 -27.03)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 77.3)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.53)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 98.08 0) translate(95,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -90.76 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:12.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.75" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.29)" width="167.01"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex50.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,m)\otimes\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(m,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝔅</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,m)\otimes\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(m,k)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 437.55 0) translate(81.65,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -43.46 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.75" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.29)" width="72.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex50.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,k)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 65.68)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(193.08,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -188.84 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:26.25em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.53em;font-size:10pt;" height="19.13" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="363.17"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex50.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Hom_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n},(sV)^{\otimes m})\otimes\Hom_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes m},(sV)^{\otimes k})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Hom_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n},(sV)^{\otimes m})\otimes\Hom_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes m},(sV)^{\otimes k})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 386.17 0) translate(133.03,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -94.84 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:12.66em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.53em;font-size:10pt;" height="19.13" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="175.17"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex50.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Hom_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n},(sV)^{\otimes k}),}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\Hom_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n},(sV)^{\otimes k}),}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -20.78 25.58 L 161.52 25.58"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 161.8 25.58)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -116.06 14.14 L -116.06 -10.21"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -116.06 -10.49)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 210.05 14.14 L 210.05 -10.21"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 210.05 -10.49)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 77.3 -23.57 L 110.14 -23.57"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 110.42 -23.57)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the vertical maps are surjective.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">After the previous proposition, we only need to explain surjectivity. Surjectivity is equivalent to the statement that the space of symplectic invariant tensors inside <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sV)^{\otimes 2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sV)^{\otimes 2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by the classes obtained by inserting the symplectic form <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times, which follows from Weyl’s decomposition of <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes 2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes 2n}</annotation></semantics></math> into irreducible representations described in the previous section. Alternatively, surjectivity is part of the first fundamental theorem of invariant theory for the symplectic group.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the Brauer algebra <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> surjects onto the centralizer <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, there is an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n})\cong\End_{\Sp(V)}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n})\cong\End_{\Sp(V)}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> given by desuspending and carefully inserting signs. This isomorphism is described explicitly by Hanlon and Wales <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">HW89</a>, Theorem 2.10]</cite>. Let us explain why their result gives such an isomorphism. Hanlon and Wales define two versions of Brauer algebra, <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{f}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{f}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{B}_{f}^{(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔅</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{B}_{f}^{(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any natural number <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and any parameter <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the ground field. These algebras act naturally on the <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>th tensor power of a vector space of dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a symmetric or antisymmetric bilinear form, respectively. They show by a direct calculation that there is an isomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{f}^{(x)}\cong\mathfrak{B}_{f}^{(-x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔅</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{f}^{(x)}\cong\mathfrak{B}_{f}^{(-x)}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>: in our terms, this isomorphism arises from the fact that the functor <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\mapsto sV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\mapsto sV</annotation></semantics></math> maps a vector space of dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to a space of dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-x</annotation></semantics></math>, and converts a symmetric bilinear form to an antisymmetric one and vice versa. Our algebra <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is identical with their <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{A}_{n}^{(-2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔄</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{A}_{n}^{(-2g)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The Brauer algebra contains the group algebra <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> — as it should, since the centralizer of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(V)</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> should contain the centralizer of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math> — as the subalgebra consisting of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagrams in which all strands are vertical, i.e. go from the top row to the bottom row. The inclusion <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\to\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\to\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> has a left inverse, given by mapping any diagram containing a horizontal strand to zero; one checks that the subspace spanned by all diagrams containing a horizontal strand is an ideal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Symplectic Schur–Weyl duality)<span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic vector space of dimension <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The image of the Brauer algebra </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the centralizer of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(V)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and vice versa.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv 2\pmod{n}\end{subarray}}V_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv 2\pmod{n}\end{subarray}}V_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes the simple module over the Brauer algebra </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> corresponding to </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the representation </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> coincides with the representation </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, considered as a module over the Brauer algebra via the map </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)\to\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)\to\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which sends any diagram containing a horizontal strand to zero.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Part (2) follows from (1), given a description of how the split semisimple algebra <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(V)}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(V)}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes into simple algebras <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen88</a>, Corollary 3.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It may seem strange that the representation <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, appears in part (3) of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Symplectic Schur–Weyl duality). ‣ 3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. Indeed, we have seen from Weyl’s construction that <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, when decomposed into irreducible representations of <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(V)\times\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(V)\times\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, should contain the summands <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math>. But Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Symplectic Schur–Weyl duality). ‣ 3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> says that <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the summands <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\lambda,n}\cong\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\lambda,n}\cong\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math>. So why isn’t this a contradiction? The reason is that when <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> via the composition</p>
<table id="S3.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\hookrightarrow\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)\to\End_{\mathbf{Q}}((sV)^{\otimes n})\cong\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(V^{\otimes n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\hookrightarrow\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)\to\End_{\mathbf{Q}}((sV)^{\otimes n})\cong\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(V^{\otimes n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then this action is <em id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> equal to the standard action of <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> by permuting the factors; instead, one obtains the standard action twisted by the sign representation. So the isomorphism <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\lambda,n}\cong\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\lambda,n}\cong\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> does hold when <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is considered as a <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>-module by restriction of scalars, but this is not the same as the <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>-module structure obtained by the natural action of <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The conventions are more natural when <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is placed in odd degree: the composition <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\to\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)\to\End_{\mathbf{Q}}((sV)^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\to\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)\to\End_{\mathbf{Q}}((sV)^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> does give the standard action of <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sV)^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sV)^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which now takes the Koszul sign rule into account. Thus <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sV)^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sV)^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> will contain <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> as a summand, placed in odd/even degree according to whether <math id="S3.Thmthm6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd/even. We caution the reader that the calculations of this paper will require some care to be taken to tensor with the sign representation when appropriate, in particular when passing between Chow groups and cohomology groups.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we want to decompose <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> into irreducible representations of <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(V)\times\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(V)\times\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we may start from the usual Schur–Weyl duality (which gives a decomposition into irreducible representations of <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\GL(V)\times\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\GL(V)\times\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>), and apply a branching formula for <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(V)\subset\GL(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(V)\subset\GL(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently we could start with the symplectic
Schur–Weyl duality (which gives a decomposition into <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(V)\times\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝔅</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(V)\times\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-representations) and try to determine how the modules <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mu,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mu,n}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> decompose into sums of Specht modules under restriction of scalars to <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>: note that if</p>
<table id="S3.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Res^{\GL(V)}_{\Sp(V)}V_{\lambda}\cong\bigoplus_{\mu}a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\,\,V_{\langle\mu\rangle}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Res</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>GL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>μ</mi></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Res^{\GL(V)}_{\Sp(V)}V_{\lambda}\cong\bigoplus_{\mu}a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\,\,V_{\langle\mu\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for some integers <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\mu}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\mu}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\mu,n}\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\,\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\mu,n}\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\,\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Then the decomposition of <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> reads</p>
<table id="S3.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\bigoplus_{\mu}a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\,\,V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>μ</mi></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\bigoplus_{\mu}a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\,\,V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The discussion in the preceding paragraphs says that that <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\mu}^{\lambda}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> only for <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu|\equiv|\lambda|\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu|\equiv|\lambda|\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu|&lt;|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu|&lt;|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>, with the sole exception of <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\lambda}^{\lambda}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\lambda}^{\lambda}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The problem of calculating the coefficients <math id="S3.Thmthm7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\mu}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\mu}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> was first solved by Littlewood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lit40</a>]</cite> and Newell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">New51</a>]</cite>, and many subsequent authors have given methods for computing them.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Projectors</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the above, it is natural to ask for an analogue of Young symmetrizers in the Brauer algebra. That is, one would like idempotents <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}\in\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}\in\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the image of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the irreducible summand <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>. The question of how to find such idempotents <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> was posed already by Weyl. Nevertheless, no explicit construction was known until Nazarov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naz04</a>]</cite> gave a simple formula describing <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the most interesting case <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math>. Although the statement of the result is elementary and involves only very classical representation theory, the proof proceeds through the theory of quantum groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results of Section <a href="#S10" title="10. Low genus calculations ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, and some of the examples in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, rely on computer calculations which require us to have explicit formulas for the idempotents <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the reader does not need to know the precise expression for <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to follow the arguments, only that such a formula exists. Nevertheless we state Nazarov’s theorem here for completeness. For a partition <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the <em id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">row tableau</em> associated to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to be the Young tableau given by filling in the numbers <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> in the Ferrers diagram so that the first row gets the numbers <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,2,\ldots,\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,2,\ldots,\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the second row gets the numbers <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}+1,\lambda_{1}+2,\ldots,\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}+1,\lambda_{1}+2,\ldots,\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on. We define the <em id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">content</em> of a box in the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th row and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th column of the Ferrers diagram to be <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-i</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the number <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the content of the box labeled “<math id="S3.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>” in the row tableau corresponding to <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i,j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i,j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> be the element of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to contracting and inserting the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th tensor factors with the symplectic form. That is, it has <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> vertical strands, and two horizontal ones: one connecting the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th “inputs”, and one connecting the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th “outputs”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nazarov)<span id="S3.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any partition <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}=\prod_{k,l}\left(1+\frac{B_{kl}}{2g+1+c_{k}(\lambda)+c_{l}(\lambda)}\right)\cdot c_{\lambda^{T}}\in\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}=\prod_{k,l}\left(1+\frac{B_{kl}}{2g+1+c_{k}(\lambda)+c_{l}(\lambda)}\right)\cdot c_{\lambda^{T}}\in\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here the product ranges over all pairs <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k&lt;l\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k&lt;l\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> such that the boxes labeled <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> are in distinct rows of the row tableau associated to <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the operators <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{kl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{kl}</annotation></semantics></math> do not commute, this must be interpreted as an <em id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">ordered</em> product: we order the terms in the product lexicographically by <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,l)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Young symmetrizer. The image of <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda,n}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(sV)^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(sV)^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the summand <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is placed in odd degree if <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and even degree if <math id="S3.Thmthm8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For our purposes it would be enough to have formulas for such idempotents in the smaller algebra <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(V)}((sV)^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>, which a priori do not have to lift to an idempotent of <math id="S3.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that in principle we could have used results of Ram and Wenzl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">RW92</a>]</cite> instead of Nazarov’s theorem.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>A result of Ancona</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Schur functors</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear symmetric monoidal category, and let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\ob\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ob</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\ob\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> has an action of the group algebra <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that there is a homomorphism of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\to\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(M^{\otimes n},M^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\to\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(M^{\otimes n},M^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us suppose moreover that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is pseudo-abelian, i.e. that every idempotent endomorphism in <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> has an image. For <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda}\in\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda}\in\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a Young symmetrizer corresponding to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}^{\lambda}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}^{\lambda}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the image of the idempotent <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Schur functor</em> corresponding to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a decomposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del02</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}=\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\mathsf{S}^{\lambda}(M)\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>λ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}=\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\mathsf{S}^{\lambda}(M)\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the representation of the symmetric group corresponding to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is the category of finite dimensional <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces, this is the decomposition of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> given by Schur–Weyl duality, described in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key fact used in proving this result is that <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a semisimple algebra and in fact a product of matrix algebras over <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>: there is an isomorphism <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\cong\prod_{|\lambda|=n}\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(\sigma_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]\cong\prod_{|\lambda|=n}\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(\sigma_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Brauer algebra action</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will need to generalize Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to a weaker notion of symmetrically self-dual object.
An object <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invertible</em> if the functor <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\otimes L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\otimes L</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalence of categories. If this is the case then <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is dualizable, the quasi-inverse is given by tensoring with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the maps <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\to L\otimes L^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\to L\otimes L^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\otimes L^{\ast}\to\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\otimes L^{\ast}\to\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphisms. We say that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">even</em> or <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">odd</em> if <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(L^{\otimes n},L^{\otimes n})\cong\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(L^{\otimes n},L^{\otimes n})\cong\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math> by the trivial representation or the sign representation, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\ob\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ob</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\ob\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weakly self-dual</em> if there is an even invertible object <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\ob\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ob</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\ob\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and unit and counit maps</p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to M\otimes M\qquad M\otimes M\to L" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to M\otimes M\qquad M\otimes M\to L</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">such that the compositions</p>
<table id="S4.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\otimes L\to M\otimes M\otimes M\to L\otimes M" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\otimes L\to M\otimes M\otimes M\to L\otimes M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\otimes M\to M\otimes M\otimes M\to M\otimes L" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\otimes M\to M\otimes M\otimes M\to M\otimes L</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">both equal the swap map. This implies that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\ast}\cong M\otimes L^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\ast}\cong M\otimes L^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S4.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">weakly symmetrically self-dual</em> if moreover the unit and counit maps are invariant under the swap map <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\otimes M\to M\otimes M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\otimes M\to M\otimes M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We omit the proof of the following result, which generalizes Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and is an exercise in the diagrammatic calculus for rigid symmetric monoidal categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a weakly symmetrically self-dual object of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of quantum dimension <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an action of <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, under which the subalgebra <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the usual way, and a Brauer diagram of the form
<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="19.61" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-5.87px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 22.29 19.61" width="22.29"><g transform="translate(0,19.61) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(-11.14,0) translate(0,1.93)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 17.13 15.75 C 17.13 16.51 16.51 17.13 15.75 17.13 C 14.99 17.13 14.37 16.51 14.37 15.75 C 14.37 14.99 14.99 14.37 15.75 14.37 C 16.51 14.37 17.13 14.99 17.13 15.75 Z M 15.75 15.75"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(0.2 0.0 0.0 0.2 14.36 15.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.02em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="14.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:1.02em;">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 17.13 0 C 17.13 0.76 16.51 1.38 15.75 1.38 C 14.99 1.38 14.37 0.76 14.37 0 C 14.37 -0.76 14.99 -1.38 15.75 -1.38 C 16.51 -1.38 17.13 -0.76 17.13 0 Z M 15.75 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(0.2 0.0 0.0 0.2 14.36 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.02em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="14.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:1.02em;">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 32.87 15.75 C 32.87 16.51 32.26 17.13 31.5 17.13 C 30.73 17.13 30.12 16.51 30.12 15.75 C 30.12 14.99 30.73 14.37 31.5 14.37 C 32.26 14.37 32.87 14.99 32.87 15.75 Z M 31.5 15.75"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(0.2 0.0 0.0 0.2 30.11 15.75)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.02em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="14.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:1.02em;">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.3.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 32.87 0 C 32.87 0.76 32.26 1.38 31.5 1.38 C 30.73 1.38 30.12 0.76 30.12 0 C 30.12 -0.76 30.73 -1.38 31.5 -1.38 C 32.26 -1.38 32.87 -0.76 32.87 0 Z M 31.5 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.8pt" transform="matrix(0.2 0.0 0.0 0.2 30.11 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.02em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="14.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:1.02em;">
<span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.pic1.4.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 31.49 14.26 C 31.5 8.12 15.75 8.12 15.75 14.26"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 15.75 1.49 C 15.75 7.63 31.5 7.63 31.49 1.49"></path></g></g></svg></span> acts as the composition</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\otimes M\to L\to M\otimes M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\otimes M\to L\to M\otimes M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A more general statement is that any element of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a well defined morphism in <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(M^{\otimes m}\otimes L^{n-m},M^{\otimes n})=\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(M^{\otimes m},M^{\otimes n}\otimes L^{m-n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(M^{\otimes m}\otimes L^{n-m},M^{\otimes n})=\Hom_{\mathcal{C}}(M^{\otimes m},M^{\otimes n}\otimes L^{m-n})</annotation></semantics></math>, in a way compatible with composition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.2. Brauer algebra action ‣ 4. A result of Ancona ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> does not in general lead to a decomposition of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> into summands indexed by irreducible representations of the Brauer algebra, even over <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason is that the algebra <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(\delta)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is not in general semisimple, which was the crucial property of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\mathfrak{S}_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> used for defining the decomposition of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of Schur functors. In the case of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, which acts naturally on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> a symplectic vector space of dimension <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>, what <em id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">does</em> hold is that <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(2g)}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(2g)}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the image of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\mathbf{Q}}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>, is a product of matrix algebras over <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Self-products of abelian schemes</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon A\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon A\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be an abelian scheme of relative dimension <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, where we assume <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> smooth and connected. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> be the local system <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}f_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by a homomorphism <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(S,x_{0})\to\Sp(2g,\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(S,x_{0})\to\Sp(2g,\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. The Brauer algebra <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold tensor power of the defining representation <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence also on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>. As explained in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Symplectic Schur–Weyl duality). ‣ 3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> the Brauer algebra action gives rise to a decomposition</p>
<table id="S4.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which then gives us also a decomposition of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The next result is a special case of the main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anc15</a>]</cite>. See also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moo16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The above decomposition of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to the category of Chow motives over <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(A/S)^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}h^{1}(A/S)_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{(n-|\lambda|)/2}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(A/S)^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}h^{1}(A/S)_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{(n-|\lambda|)/2}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the action of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to an action on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(A/S)^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(A/S)^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.2. Brauer algebra action ‣ 4. A result of Ancona ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> given that the cup product <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(A/S)\otimes h^{1}(A/S)\to\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(A/S)\otimes h^{1}(A/S)\to\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(A/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(A/S)</annotation></semantics></math> weakly symmetrically self-dual, and that <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim h^{1}(A/S)=-2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim h^{1}(A/S)=-2g</annotation></semantics></math>. To see that <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim h^{1}(A/S)=-2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim h^{1}(A/S)=-2g</annotation></semantics></math>, observe that the quantum dimension of an object is preserved by any strict symmetric monoidal functor. We apply this to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\colon\mathsf{Mot}_{S}\to\mathsf{grVect}_{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝗀𝗋𝖵𝖾𝖼𝗍</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\colon\mathsf{Mot}_{S}\to\mathsf{grVect}_{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> given by
<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(M)=\bigoplus_{i}\mathcal{H}^{i}(\mathsf{real}\,M)_{x_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝗋𝖾𝖺𝗅</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(M)=\bigoplus_{i}\mathcal{H}^{i}(\mathsf{real}\,M)_{x_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\in S</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary point. Since <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1})=\mathsf{grVect_{\mathbf{Q}}}(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1})=\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝗀𝗋𝖵𝖾𝖼𝗍</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{S}(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1})=\mathsf{grVect_{\mathbf{Q}}}(\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1})=\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim h^{1}(A/S)=\dim\omega(h^{1}(A/S))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim h^{1}(A/S)=\dim\omega(h^{1}(A/S))</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(h^{1}(A/S))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(h^{1}(A/S))</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector space <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(A_{x_{0}},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(A_{x_{0}},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> placed in degree <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, so its dimension in the sense of graded vector spaces is <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in Remark <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Remark 4.2. ‣ 4.2. Brauer algebra action ‣ 4. A result of Ancona ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, the obstruction to obtaining a result like Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4.3. Self-products of abelian schemes ‣ 4. A result of Ancona ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> in a general rigid symmetric monoidal category is that the algebra <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> does not split as a product of matrix algebras; only its quotient <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(2g)}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(2g)}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> does. The key point is then that the action of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(A/S)^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(A/S)^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> factors through <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\End_{\Sp(2g)}(V^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>End</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\End_{\Sp(2g)}(V^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>. In Ancona’s paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anc15</a>]</cite> this is proven using O’Sullivan’s results on symmetrically distinguished cycles on abelian varieties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’S11</a>]</cite>. An alternative proof (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anc12</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">LZ15</a>, Theorem 4.8]</cite>) proceeds by using invariant theory to show that the kernel of the homomorphism of PROPs of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 3.4. ‣ 3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is the PROP-ideal generated by a single element of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(g+1,g+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(g+1,g+1)</annotation></semantics></math> whose vanishing is equivalent to <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{2g+2}(V)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{2g+2}(V)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the fact that this single relation holds also on the level of Chow groups is equivalent to the fact that <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(A/S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(A/S)</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite dimensional motive in the sense of Kimura. Either way it is clear that the result is at present quite special to abelian varieties.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal genus <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curve, and <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fibered power of <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. There are <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> natural line bundles <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>; the fiber of <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over a moduli point is given by the cotangent space of the curve at the <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th marking. We denote the first Chern class of <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is pulled back from <math id="S5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> along the map <math id="S5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}\to C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}\to C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that forgets all markings except the <math id="S5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We make the definition <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{d}=p_{\ast}\psi_{1}^{d+1}\in\mathrm{CH}^{d}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{d}=p_{\ast}\psi_{1}^{d+1}\in\mathrm{CH}^{d}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{-1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}=(2g-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}=(2g-2)</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by the same symbol <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> also the pullback of this class to <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any distinct elements <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> we denote by <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> the class of the diagonal locus where the <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th marked point coincide with each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological ring</em> <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the subring of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> generated by all <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes, <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-classes and diagonal classes. The <em id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological cohomology ring</em> <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of the tautological ring inside <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> under the cycle class map. (The grading of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is twice that of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\subset H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\subset H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The generators for the tautological rings satisfy the following relations for all <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\Delta_{ij}\Delta_{ik}&amp;=\Delta_{ij}\Delta_{jk}\\
\Delta_{ij}\psi_{i}&amp;=\Delta_{ij}\psi_{j}\\
\Delta_{ij}^{2}&amp;=-\Delta_{ij}\psi_{i}\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\Delta_{ij}\Delta_{ik}&amp;=\Delta_{ij}\Delta_{jk}\\
\Delta_{ij}\psi_{i}&amp;=\Delta_{ij}\psi_{j}\\
\Delta_{ij}^{2}&amp;=-\Delta_{ij}\psi_{i}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The first two are geometrically obvious, and the third one is a consequence of the excess intersection formula.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> be the commutative graded <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra generated by classes <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> range from <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and are distinct) and <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, modulo the above three relations.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By a “commutative graded” algebra (as opposed to a “graded commutative” algebra) we mean an algebra in which <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\cdot y=y\cdot x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\cdot y=y\cdot x</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of their degree; we do not impose the Koszul sign rule <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\cdot y=(-1)^{|x|\cdot|y|}y\cdot x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\cdot y=(-1)^{|x|\cdot|y|}y\cdot x</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a natural surjection</p>
<table id="S5.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}^{\bullet}\to R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}^{\bullet}\to R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and describing the tautological ring <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to describing the kernel of this surjection. The algebra <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> plays the same role for the study of <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> as the <em id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strata algebra</em> does for the study of <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx62" title="" class="ltx_ref">PPZ15</a>, 0.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in the introduction, tautological classes are usually considered on the Deligne–Mumford spaces. In that case the tautological rings <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined, following Faber and Pandharipande <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">FP05</a>]</cite>, as the smallest collection of unital subrings of <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(\overline{M}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> closed under pushforward along the gluing maps</p>
<table id="S5.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{g,n+2}\to\overline{{M}}_{g+1,n}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{{M}}_{g,n+1}\times\overline{{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}+1}\to\overline{{M}}_{g+g^{\prime},n+n^{\prime}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{g,n+2}\to\overline{{M}}_{g+1,n}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\overline{{M}}_{g,n+1}\times\overline{{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}+1}\to\overline{{M}}_{g+g^{\prime},n+n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the forgetful maps</p>
<table id="S5.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{M}}_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{g,n+1}\to\overline{{M}}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Although it is not imposed in the definition, it turns out that the tautological rings are also closed under pullback along the same maps. A similar characterization can be given of the tautological rings <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For any function <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon\{1,\ldots,n\}\to\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon\{1,\ldots,n\}\to\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> there is a map <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{m}\to C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{m}\to C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m})\mapsto(C,x_{\phi(1)},\ldots,x_{\phi(n)})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m})\mapsto(C,x_{\phi(1)},\ldots,x_{\phi(n)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We call all maps of this form <em id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological</em>. Then it is not hard to see that the system of tautological rings <math id="S5.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined to be the smallest collection of unital subrings closed under pushforward along all tautological maps, and it turns out a posteriori that the tautological rings are also closed under pullback along the same maps. ∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>The Künneth decomposition of the universal curve</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Künneth decomposition, the summands 
          
            
              
              h
              0
            
          
         and 
          
            
              
              h
              
                ⁢
                2
                d ‣ 2. Chow motives ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, any choice of a cycle <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on each fiber of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a decomposition of the relative Chow motive:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}/M_{g})=h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})\oplus h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})\oplus h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}/M_{g})=h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})\oplus h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})\oplus h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g})=\mathbf{Q}\{\kappa_{1},\psi_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g})=\mathbf{Q}\{\kappa_{1},\psi_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on the fibers of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the only possibilities we have are <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathfrak{z}=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}+\mathrm{(const.)}\cdot\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>const</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}+\mathrm{(const.)}\cdot\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Regardless of the constant we get <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}=\mathfrak{z}-\frac{1}{2}p^{\ast}p_{\ast}\mathfrak{z}^{2}=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}^{\prime}=\mathfrak{z}-\frac{1}{2}p^{\ast}p_{\ast}\mathfrak{z}^{2}=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence without making any choices we get projectors <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by</p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex69"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex69.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Delta_{12}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Delta_{12}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex70"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We have isomorphisms <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\cong h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\cong h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}\cong h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕃</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}\cong h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unit object and the Lefschetz motive in the category of Chow motives over <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the interesting motive is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We may form the Chow groups of these relative motives: there is an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S5.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})=\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h(C_{g}/M_{g}))=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2}\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})=\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h(C_{g}/M_{g}))=\bigoplus_{i=0}^{2}\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex72"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex72.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Im}(\pi_{0}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Im}(\pi_{0}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex73"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex73.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Im}(\pi_{1}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Im}(\pi_{1}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex74"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex74.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Im}(\pi_{2}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k-1}(M_{g})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Im</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Im}(\pi_{2}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k-1}(M_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The isomorphism <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by the pullback <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the isomorphism <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k-1}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k-1}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> by the proper pushforward <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>. Informally, the Chow groups of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> capture the parts of the Chow groups of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that do not come from the base <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us consider the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold fibered power <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{n}_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{n}_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})=h(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})=h(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so our decomposition yields an equally canonical isomorphism</p>
<table id="S5.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})=\bigoplus_{i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}}\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})=\bigoplus_{i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}}\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We call this the <em id="S5.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative Künneth decomposition</em> of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. By extension, we will also refer to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})=\bigoplus_{i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}}\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})=\bigoplus_{i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}}\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> as the relative Künneth decomposition of the Chow groups of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> we get a projector <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> acting on <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> with image <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular this projector acts by correspondences on <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with image <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\bigotimes_{j=1}^{n}h^{i_{j}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> for the projector <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\times\pi_{1}\times\ldots\times\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\times\pi_{1}\times\ldots\times\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Tautological maps</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider how the decomposition just defined behaves under the tautological maps between the moduli spaces <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1. </span>Cross product</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The isomorphism <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes h(C_{g}^{m}/M_{g})\cong h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes h(C_{g}^{m}/M_{g})\cong h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> yields <em id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross product</em> maps</p>
<table id="S5.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes\mathrm{CH}^{l}(C_{g}^{m})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k+l}(C_{g}^{n+m});" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes\mathrm{CH}^{l}(C_{g}^{m})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k+l}(C_{g}^{n+m});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">explicitly, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\times\beta=\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{\ast}(\alpha)\cdot\mathrm{pr}_{2}^{\ast}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>pr</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\times\beta=\mathrm{pr}_{1}^{\ast}(\alpha)\cdot\mathrm{pr}_{2}^{\ast}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pr}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pr}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pr}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>pr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pr}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote projections onto the first <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and last <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> factors, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the relative Künneth decomposition of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n+m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n+m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tensor product of the relative Künneth decompositions of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that the cross product maps are compatible with the projectors <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in a strong sense: for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1},\ldots,j_{m}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1},\ldots,j_{m}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S5.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha\times(\pi_{j_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{j_{m}})\circ\beta=(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\pi_{i_{n}}\times\pi_{j_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{j_{m}})\circ(\alpha\times\beta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha\times(\pi_{j_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{j_{m}})\circ\beta=(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\pi_{i_{n}}\times\pi_{j_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{j_{m}})\circ(\alpha\times\beta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2. </span>Forgetful maps</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\colon C_{g}^{n+m}\to C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\colon C_{g}^{n+m}\to C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the map that forgets the last <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> markings. Considering <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as a correspondence gives maps of Chow motives</p>
<table id="S5.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\qquad\text{and}\qquad h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\qquad\text{and}\qquad h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which upon taking Chow groups gives the maps</p>
<table id="S5.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\ast}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})\qquad\text{and}\qquad p_{\ast}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k-m}(C_{g}^{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\ast}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})\qquad\text{and}\qquad p_{\ast}\colon\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k-m}(C_{g}^{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Now the map <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the composition <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\cong h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m}\subset h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\cong h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m}\subset h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the composition <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\twoheadrightarrow h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m}\cong h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n+m}/M_{g})\twoheadrightarrow h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m}\cong h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that the maps <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> are also compatible with the relative Künneth decomposition of Chow groups:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">—</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by mapping each summand <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphically onto the summand <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}}\times\pi_{0}\times\ldots\times\pi_{0})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}}\times\pi_{0}\times\ldots\times\pi_{0})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})</annotation></semantics></math>. This can also be seen from the fact that <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by cross product with the class <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in\mathrm{CH}^{0}(C_{g}^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in\mathrm{CH}^{0}(C_{g}^{m})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">—</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> maps each summand <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}}\times\pi_{2}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}}\times\pi_{2}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n+m})</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphically onto the summand <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k-m}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\mathrm{CH}^{k-m}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes on all summands not of this form.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.3. </span>Diagonals</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diagonal <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}\to C_{g}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}\to C_{g}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, considered as a correspondence, defines a map of Chow motives <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the cup product on the level of Chow motives. Forming the relative Künneth decomposition on both sides, we see that the cup product is the sum of maps <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{j}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{k}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{j}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{k}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We caution the reader that this is <em id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> in general a multiplicative decomposition, in the sense of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi14</a>]</cite>: that is, the maps <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{j}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{k}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{j}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{k}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> only nonzero for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+j=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+j=k</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, note that if <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\subset C_{g}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\subset C_{g}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the small diagonal, considered as a correspondence <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{2}\vdash C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>⊢</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{2}\vdash C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the decomposition is multiplicative if and only if</p>
<table id="S5.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{k}\circ\delta\circ(\pi_{i}\times\pi_{j})=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{k}\circ\delta\circ(\pi_{i}\times\pi_{j})=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+j\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+j\neq k</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we have</p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex81"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{k}\circ\delta\circ(\pi_{i}\times\pi_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{k}\circ\delta\circ(\pi_{i}\times\pi_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex81.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(p_{126})_{\ast}(p_{13}^{\ast}(\pi_{i})\cdot p_{24}^{\ast}(\pi_{j})\cdot\Delta_{345}\cdot p_{56}^{\ast}(\pi_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>126</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>13</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>24</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>345</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>56</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(p_{126})_{\ast}(p_{13}^{\ast}(\pi_{i})\cdot p_{24}^{\ast}(\pi_{j})\cdot\Delta_{345}\cdot p_{56}^{\ast}(\pi_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex82"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(p_{456})_{\ast}(\Delta_{123}\cdot p_{14}^{\ast}(\pi_{i}^{t})\cdot p_{25}^{\ast}(\pi_{j}^{t})\cdot p_{46}^{\ast}(\pi_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>456</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>14</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>25</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>46</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(p_{456})_{\ast}(\Delta_{123}\cdot p_{14}^{\ast}(\pi_{i}^{t})\cdot p_{25}^{\ast}(\pi_{j}^{t})\cdot p_{46}^{\ast}(\pi_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex83"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\pi_{2-i}\times\pi_{2-j}\times\pi_{k})\circ\Delta_{123}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\pi_{2-i}\times\pi_{2-j}\times\pi_{k})\circ\Delta_{123}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Thus we get a more symmetric condition for the decomposition to be multiplicative: we must have <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{a}\times\pi_{b}\times\pi_{c})\circ\Delta_{123}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{a}\times\pi_{b}\times\pi_{c})\circ\Delta_{123}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b+c\neq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b+c\neq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, e.g. the nonvanishing of the Gross–Schoen cycle (Example <a href="#S6.SS4" title="6.4. Example: the Gross–Schoen cycle. ‣ 6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>) implies that the decomposition is not multiplicative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, let us also remark that when <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we do have that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{a}\times\pi_{b}\times\pi_{c})\circ\Delta_{123}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{a}\times\pi_{b}\times\pi_{c})\circ\Delta_{123}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b+c\neq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b+c\neq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, and the decomposition is multiplicative. More generally, it follows from the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tav18b</a>]</cite> that the decomposition is multiplicative over the moduli space of <em id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hyperelliptic</em> curves of arbitrary genus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In any case, failure of decomposition to be multiplicative implies that the cup product in the algebra <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> will look somewhat strange with respect to the relative Künneth decomposition of the Chow groups of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. The situation is analogous to the what happens in topology, when one has a multiplicative spectral sequence <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}^{pq}\implies H^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}^{pq}\implies H^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the cup product on the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> page of the spectral sequence is different from the cup product in the algebra <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the level of Betti realizations, this is more than an analogy. The cohomology groups <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> carry a <em id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Leray filtration</em>, and the associated graded <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> page of the Leray spectral sequence for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our canonical decomposition of the Chow motive <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> gives, on the level of cohomology, an isomorphism of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\cong\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\cong\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. But this is not an isomorphism of algebras: the multiplication in <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by using only the maps <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{j}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{k}(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{j}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{k}(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+j=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+j=k</annotation></semantics></math>, and discarding all other parts of the cup product <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes 2}\to h(C_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes 2}\to h(C_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Decomposition into representations of the symplectic group</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal jacobian. By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 2.6. ‣ 2.4. Künneth decomposition for abelian schemes ‣ 2. Chow motives ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> there is an isomorphism of Chow motives over <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong h^{1}(J_{g}/M_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong h^{1}(J_{g}/M_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It follows that Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4.3. Self-products of abelian schemes ‣ 4. A result of Ancona ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> gives us a decomposition</p>
<table id="S5.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{n-|\lambda|}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}\cong\bigoplus_{\begin{subarray}{c}|\lambda|\leq n\\
|\lambda|\equiv n\pmod{2}\end{subarray}}h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{n-|\lambda|}\otimes\beta_{\lambda,n}^{\ast}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We denote the motive <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. We often write <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> for the motive <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also denote by <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> the summand of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we refer to this as the <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">primitive part</em> of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One can make the action of the Brauer algebra <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> more explicit.
Let <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagram. Label the nodes in the Brauer diagram along the top row as <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> and along the bottom row as <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1,\ldots,2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1,\ldots,2n</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ij)\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ij)\in B</annotation></semantics></math> to denote that the <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th row are connected by a strand. Then</p>
<table id="S5.Ex86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{(ij)\in B}p_{ij}^{\ast}(\pi_{1})\in\mathrm{CH}^{n}(C_{g}^{2n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{(ij)\in B}p_{ij}^{\ast}(\pi_{1})\in\mathrm{CH}^{n}(C_{g}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is a well defined correspondence <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}\vdash C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>⊢</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}\vdash C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we consider <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as a cycle in <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the projection onto the <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th factor. This correspondence gives a map <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> that preserves the summand <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we obtain a well defined action of <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This action agrees with the one defined in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.2. Brauer algebra action ‣ 4. A result of Ancona ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the level of Chow groups, we can also describe the action of a Brauer diagram as follows. An <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagram in which the <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> dot on the top row are connected by a strand, and all others are vertical, gives rise to the following morphism of Chow groups:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})\hookrightarrow\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\Delta_{ij}^{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n-1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet-1}(C_{g}^{n-2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})\hookrightarrow\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\Delta_{ij}^{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n-1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p_{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet-1}(C_{g}^{n-2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the projection that forgets the marked point corresponding to the diagonal <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. The image of the composition of these morphisms actually lands inside the summand <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet-1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n-2})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet-1}(C_{g}^{n-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet-1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n-2})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet-1}(C_{g}^{n-2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, an <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-2,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-2,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagram in which the <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th and <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th dot on the bottom row are connected by a strand gives rise to the following morphism of Chow groups:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes(n-2)})\hookrightarrow\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n-2})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p^{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n-1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle(\Delta_{ij})_{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet+1}(C_{g}^{n})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi_{1}^{\times n}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet+1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mover><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes(n-2)})\hookrightarrow\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n-2})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle p^{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n-1})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle(\Delta_{ij})_{\ast}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet+1}(C_{g}^{n})\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi_{1}^{\times n}}}{{\longrightarrow}}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet+1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4. </span>Decomposing the tautological ring</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have explained that <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of terms of the form <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{0}}\otimes h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{1}}\otimes h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{0}}\otimes h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{1}}\otimes h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}+n_{1}+n_{2}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}+n_{1}+n_{2}=n</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we also have <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of terms of the form <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{n_{1}-|\lambda|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{n_{1}-|\lambda|}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we conclude that <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of motives of the form <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> and their Tate twists.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary, and consider <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose any decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Mot}_{M_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Mot}_{M_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then under the equality <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> we have the following compatibility:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex90" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="84.16" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-42.08px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 261.68 84.16" width="261.68"><g transform="translate(0,84.16) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(130.84,0) translate(0,42.08)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -130.84 -23.18)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 84.15)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(41.39,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.43 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="56.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 82.78 0) translate(97.94,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -75.01 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="135.5"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}\mathrm{CH}^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}\mathrm{CH}^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 65.25)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 4.88 0) translate(36.51,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -30.55 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="46.6"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 85.74 0) translate(94.98,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -72.05 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.37em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="129.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}R^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}),}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}R^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}),}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.76 25.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cong" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cong</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -85.99 -0.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.8)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subseteq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subseteq</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -45.72 -23.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cong" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cong</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 53.34 -5.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.8)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex90.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subseteq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subseteq</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where both horizontal arrows are induced by our choice of decomposition of <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a summand <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n_{1}}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n_{1}}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and a summand <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n_{2}}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n_{2}}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a correspondence <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\mathrm{CH}(C_{g}^{n_{1}+n_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>CH</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\mathrm{CH}(C_{g}^{n_{1}+n_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> — not a correspondence of degree <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, in general — which maps the first summand isomorphically onto the second, considered as a correspondence <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n_{1}}\vdash C_{g}^{n_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>⊢</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n_{1}}\vdash C_{g}^{n_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> can be built out of the projectors onto the Künneth components of <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and the correspondences given by Brauer diagrams. As such, <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is actually a tautological class.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a summand of <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> and as such there is a well defined subspace of tautological classes inside it, which we denote <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly for the other summand. Now the correspondence <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> which gives the isomorphism between the two summands is a tautological class, and in particular it maps tautological classes to tautological classes and gives an isomorphism (not preserving the grading unless <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}=m_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}=m_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}})\cong R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{1}})\cong R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{{\langle\lambda\rangle}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally since the projectors onto each summand <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are all given by tautological classes, the tautological ring <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of its projections onto each of the summands in the decomposition of <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5. </span>Curves with rational tails</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth projective variety, and <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[n]</annotation></semantics></math> the Fulton–MacPherson compactification of the configuration space of <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct ordered points on <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">FM94</a>]</cite>. A result of Li <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li09</a>]</cite> expresses the Chow motive <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X[n])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X[n])</annotation></semantics></math> as a direct sum of Chow motives of the form <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(X)^{\otimes i}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(X)^{\otimes i}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>; this can be seen by an inductive argument from the blow-up formula and the construction of <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X[n]</annotation></semantics></math> as an iterated blow-up of the cartesian product <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The analogous statement remains true (with the same proof) for a family <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to S</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth projective varieties, and the relative Chow motive of the relative Fulton–MacPherson compactification. In particular we may consider the universal family <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> over the moduli space of curves. In this case the relative configuration space of <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinct ordered points is the space <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the relative Fulton–MacPherson compactification of <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math> of curves with <em id="S5.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rational tails</em>, which is an iterated blow-up of <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. By “relative compactification” we mean that the map <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from the above considerations that the results of this section remain valid when <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced with <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There will in particular exist a decomposition of Chow motives</p>
<table id="S5.Ex91" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}/M_{g})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}/M_{g})\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m_{i}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and moreover, there is a canonical choice of such decomposition in which each correspondence projecting onto a summand is a tautological class (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li09</a>, Theorem 3.2]</cite>). It follows in particular that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex92" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}})\cong\bigoplus_{i}R^{\bullet-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}})\cong\bigoplus_{i}R^{\bullet-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li09</a>]</cite> give explicit formulas expressing the motive <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of motives <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\otimes i}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\otimes i}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence in terms of Tate twists of motives <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. For an <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant version (formulated in that paper only in terms of cohomology) see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Get95</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6. </span>Tautological cohomology groups</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a partition <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-local system on <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by the representation of <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> of highest weight <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the Chow motive <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> has as its Betti realization <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}[-|\lambda|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}[-|\lambda|]</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the local system <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, considered as a complex concentrated in cohomological degree <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is a cycle class map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to H^{2k-|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to H^{2k-|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> the image of <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> under the cycle class map. For <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> we get the usual tautological cohomology groups of <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. A folklore conjecture says that any homological equivalence between tautological classes is a rational equivalence, which would imply that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex94" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{2k-|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{2k-|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is always an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We caution the reader that since the Betti realization of <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is not the local system <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}[-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}[-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, there are many opportunities to get confused about the Koszul sign rule when comparing results in cohomology and in Chow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All the results of this section are valid mutatis mutandis also on the level of cohomology. One can either see this formally by applying the Betti realization functor or by repeating the proofs in the cohomological setting. For example, let us state the cohomological version of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 5.5. ‣ 5.4. Decomposing the tautological ring ‣ 5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> be arbitrary, and consider <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon C_{g}^{n}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex95" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}[-m_{i}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}\cong\bigoplus_{i}\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}[-m_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{b}_{c}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{b}_{c}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Under the equality <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\cong\mathbb{H}^{k}(M_{g},Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\cong\mathbb{H}^{k}(M_{g},Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> we have the following compatibility:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="84.16" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-42.08px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 274.1 84.16" width="274.1"><g transform="translate(0,84.16) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(137.05,0) translate(0,42.08)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -137.05 -23.18)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 84.15)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(45.42,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -39.46 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="64.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces H^{k}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 97.72 0) translate(93.24,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -70.31 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.11em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="126.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}H^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}H^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 65.25)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 2.94 0) translate(42.48,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.52 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="58.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 90.83 0) translate(100.12,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -77.19 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.11em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="25.59" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="139.87"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}),}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{i}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{k-m_{i}}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda_{i}\rangle}),}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -39.48 25.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cong" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cong</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -88.17 -0.44)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.8)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subseteq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subseteq</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -44.39 -23.18)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cong" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cong</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.39998pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 57.36 -5.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="10.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.8)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex96.m1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subseteq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subseteq</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where both horizontal arrows are induced by the choice of decomposition of <math id="S5.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rf_{\ast}\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Examples</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Example: <math id="S6.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the class <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}^{n}\in\mathrm{CH}^{n}(C_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}^{n}\in\mathrm{CH}^{n}(C_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Its image under the projectors <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S6.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex97"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{0}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{0}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex97.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(p_{2})_{\ast}(\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n+1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\psi_{1}^{n})=\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\kappa_{n-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(p_{2})_{\ast}(\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n+1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\psi_{1}^{n})=\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\kappa_{n-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex98"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex98.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(p_{2})_{\ast}(\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}^{n}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n+1}+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\psi_{1}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(p_{2})_{\ast}(\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}^{n}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n+1}+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\psi_{1}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex99"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\psi_{1}^{n}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n-1}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n}+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\kappa_{n-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\psi_{1}^{n}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n-1}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n}+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\kappa_{n-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex100"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{2}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{2}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex100.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(p_{2})_{\ast}(\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\psi_{1}^{n})=\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n-1}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\kappa_{n-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(p_{2})_{\ast}(\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}^{n}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\psi_{1}^{n})=\frac{1}{2g-2}\kappa_{n-1}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}\kappa_{n-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}\colon C_{g}^{2}\to C_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}\colon C_{g}^{2}\to C_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> forgets the <em id="S6.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">first</em> marked point. These are thus the projections of the class <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}^{n}\in\mathrm{CH}^{n}(C_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}^{n}\in\mathrm{CH}^{n}(C_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> into the three summands <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We can make some simple observations/sanity checks:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">—</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three classes sum to <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">—</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The class in <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> is pulled back from <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{n}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">—</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the class in <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g}))</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to a cycle of degree <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of a fiber of <math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">—</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The classes <math id="S6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> push forward to zero under <math id="S6.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon C_{g}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The class <math id="S6.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{2}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{2}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same pushforward as <math id="S6.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We note that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, using that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{0}=2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{0}=2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. This could also have been seen from the fact that</p>
<table id="S6.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex101"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex101.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{1})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{1})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex102"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k-1}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})\oplus\mathrm{CH}^{k-1}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which (using Harer’s calculation of the Picard groups of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har83</a>]</cite>) implies that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{0}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{0}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The first of these classes which can be nontrivial is thus <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. This class vanishes for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, but is nonzero if <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To prove that the class is nonzero for <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> it is easiest to work in cohomology. For large <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, nontriviality is a consequence of Harer stability and the Mumford conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">MW07</a>]</cite>: as <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> stabilizes to a polynomial ring in the <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-classes and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo96</a>, Proposition 2.1]</cite>. More precisely, <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4}(C_{g}^{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4}(C_{g}^{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is in the stable range when <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, so there are no relations between the generators in this degree and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> must be nonzero. Nontriviality for <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5,6</annotation></semantics></math> can be checked e.g. by multiplying the class with <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and pushing it down to <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. One computes that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\ast}(\psi_{1}^{2}\cdot(\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}))=\kappa_{3}-\frac{2}{2g-2}\kappa_{2}\kappa_{1}+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\ast}(\psi_{1}^{2}\cdot(\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}))=\kappa_{3}-\frac{2}{2g-2}\kappa_{2}\kappa_{1}+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This class can then be multiplied with <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}^{g-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}^{g-5}</annotation></semantics></math> to get a class in the socle of the tautological ring, which can be verified to be nonzero by integrating it against <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. (We discuss the <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-pairing more in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The vanishing for <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> can be proven by standard methods for computing tautological rings and a dimension count. Let us consider the case <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>: in this case one only needs to know that <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}(M_{4})\cong R^{2}(M_{4})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}(M_{4})\cong R^{2}(M_{4})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(C_{4}^{1})\cong\mathbf{Q}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(C_{4}^{1})\cong\mathbf{Q}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we have the relative Künneth decomposition</p>
<table id="S6.Ex104" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(C_{4})=R^{2}(M_{4})\oplus R^{2}(M_{4},\mathbf{V})\oplus R^{1}(M_{4})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(C_{4})=R^{2}(M_{4})\oplus R^{2}(M_{4},\mathbf{V})\oplus R^{1}(M_{4}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the first and last terms are one-dimensional since <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}(M_{4})\cong R^{2}(M_{4})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}(M_{4})\cong R^{2}(M_{4})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>; consequently, the middle term has to vanish since <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(C_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(C_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> is two-dimensional. But <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}\circ\psi_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is an element of <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(M_{4},\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(M_{4},\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>, so it must vanish.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Example: the diagonal.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us decompose the class <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12}\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12}\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> into summands. One finds exactly three nonzero terms:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex105"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\pi_{2}\times\pi_{0})\circ\Delta_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\pi_{2}\times\pi_{0})\circ\Delta_{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex105.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex105.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}\otimes\mathbf{1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝕃</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}\otimes\mathbf{1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex106"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex106.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\pi_{1}\times\pi_{1})\circ\Delta_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\pi_{1}\times\pi_{1})\circ\Delta_{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex106.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Delta_{12}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Delta_{12}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex106.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}\otimes\mathbf{V})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}\otimes\mathbf{V}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex107"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex107.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\pi_{0}\times\pi_{2})\circ\Delta_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\pi_{0}\times\pi_{2})\circ\Delta_{12}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex107.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex107.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbb{L})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbb{L}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})=\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})=\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})=\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})=\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the terms in the decomposition of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> are given exactly by the projectors <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> themselves, considered as classes on <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\otimes\mathbf{V}\cong\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2\rangle}\oplus\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle}\oplus\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\otimes\mathbf{V}\cong\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2\rangle}\oplus\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle}\oplus\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact we have <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2\rangle})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2\rangle})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the class <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generator for the summand <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{0}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{0}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The fact that <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2\rangle})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2\rangle})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})=0</annotation></semantics></math> can be proven by a dimension count argument much like the one from the previous example, using <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})=\mathbf{Q}\{\kappa_{1},\Delta_{12},\psi_{1},\psi_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})=\mathbf{Q}\{\kappa_{1},\Delta_{12},\psi_{1},\psi_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows from Harer’s calculation of the Picard group of <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har83</a>]</cite>. Now in the relative Künneth decomposition of <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(C_{g}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> we find the summands <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbf{1})\cong\mathbf{Q}\{\kappa_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbf{1})\cong\mathbf{Q}\{\kappa_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbb{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbb{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}\otimes\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝕃</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}\otimes\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}\otimes\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}\otimes\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. All these last three terms are nonzero since <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{0}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{0}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Example: the Faber–Pandharipande cycle.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the class <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(C_{g}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(C_{g}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying the operator <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times 2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the class</p>
<table id="S6.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\pi_{1}^{\times 2}\circ\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}&amp;=\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}^{2}+\psi_{2}^{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})\\
&amp;+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{2}-\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{1}^{2}\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\pi_{1}^{\times 2}\circ\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}&amp;=\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}^{2}+\psi_{2}^{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})\\
&amp;+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{2}-\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{1}^{2}\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which now defines an element of <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the class (<a href="#S6.E6" title="In 6.3. Example: the Faber–Pandharipande cycle. ‣ 6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant, it is in fact a class in <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\Sym^{2}\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\Sym^{2}\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call this class the <em id="S6.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unrefined Faber–Pandharipande cycle</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we have a decomposition <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{2}\mathbf{V}=\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle}\oplus\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{2}\mathbf{V}=\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle}\oplus\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. So (<a href="#S6.E6" title="In 6.3. Example: the Faber–Pandharipande cycle. ‣ 6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) can be written as the sum of a class in <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> and a class in <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\cong\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. As explained in Subsection <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, Nazarov’s theorem gives a general method to write down a projector acting on <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math> whose image is a particular summand <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a partition of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Although Nazarov’s theorem is overkill in this case, where one could quite easily figure out the right projector by hand, the result is that the correspondence</p>
<table id="S6.Ex108" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex108.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\frac{1}{2}(b_{13}b_{24}+b_{14}b_{23})+\frac{1}{2g}b_{12}b_{34}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(C_{g}^{4})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>24</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>14</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>34</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\frac{1}{2}(b_{13}b_{24}+b_{14}b_{23})+\frac{1}{2g}b_{12}b_{34}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(C_{g}^{4})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">acts on <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})</annotation></semantics></math> with image <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{2}^{\ast}\cong\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{2}^{\ast}\cong\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the class <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{i}+\psi_{j})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{i}+\psi_{j})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the pullback of the correspondence defining the projector <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> gives the class</p>
<table id="S6.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}^{2}+\psi_{2}^{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{2}\\
-\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{1}^{2}-\frac{2g-1}{2g(2g-2)}\kappa_{1}(\Delta_{12}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1})\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}^{2}+\psi_{2}^{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{2}\\
-\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{1}^{2}-\frac{2g-1}{2g(2g-2)}\kappa_{1}(\Delta_{12}-\frac{1}{2g-2}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2})+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which now defines an element of <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call (<a href="#S6.E7" title="In 6.3. Example: the Faber–Pandharipande cycle. ‣ 6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) the <em id="S6.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refined Faber–Pandharipande cycle</em>. This is the projection of <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the summand <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. Comparing the expressions for the refined and unrefined Faber–Pandharipande cycles, we see that we have subtracted the term</p>
<table id="S6.Ex109" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex109.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2g-1}{2g(2g-2)}\kappa_{1}b_{12}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2g-1}{2g(2g-2)}\kappa_{1}b_{12}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">This term is thus the projection of <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the summand <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\Sym^{2}\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\Sym^{2}\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>. We remark that since we know from the previous example that <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the summand <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1}b_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1}b_{12}</annotation></semantics></math> indeed gives a class in <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{L})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve of genus <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. It is interesting to consider the image of the Faber–Pandharipande cycle in <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, removing terms which obviously vanish leaves</p>
<table id="S6.Ex110" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex110.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12}\psi_{1}-\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This simplified expression is what is more commonly referred to as the Faber–Pandharipande cycle. Green and Griffiths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">GG03</a>]</cite> proved that the Faber–Pandharipande cycle is nonzero in <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for a very general curve <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> over the complex numbers. The third author gave a different proof of this result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin15</a>]</cite> valid also in positive characteristic. Such a result is rather subtle since the Faber–Pandharipande cycle is not only homologically trivial but Abel–Jacobi trivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning as in Example <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1. Example: 
          
            
              
              
                
                ψ
                1
              
              n
            
          
        . ‣ 6. Examples ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, one can show that the refined Faber–Pandharipande cycle is zero for <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2,3</annotation></semantics></math> but that it is nonzero for all <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the refined Faber–Pandharipande cycle is nonzero precisely in those genera where it is nonzero in <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for a generic curve <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. By contrast the unrefined Faber–Pandharipande cycle is nonzero also for <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, even though <math id="S6.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}(M_{3},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}(M_{3},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1\rangle})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This illustrates the utility of Nazarov’s refined projectors when trying to determine precisely which of these local systems have nonzero tautological groups.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4. </span>Example: the Gross–Schoen cycle.</h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the class <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{123}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(C_{g}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{123}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(C_{g}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. We can apply <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> to this class to get</p>
<table id="S6.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex111"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex111.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{1}^{\times 3}\circ\Delta_{123}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{1}^{\times 3}\circ\Delta_{123}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex111.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Delta_{123}-\frac{1}{2g-2}((\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{1}+(\Delta_{13}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{2}+(\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{13})\psi_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>13</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Delta_{123}-\frac{1}{2g-2}((\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{1}+(\Delta_{13}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{2}+(\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{13})\psi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex112"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}(\psi_{1}^{2}+\psi_{2}^{2}+\psi_{3}^{2})+\frac{2}{(2g-2)^{2}}(\psi_{1}\psi_{2}+\psi_{1}\psi_{3}+\psi_{2}\psi_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}(\psi_{1}^{2}+\psi_{2}^{2}+\psi_{3}^{2})+\frac{2}{(2g-2)^{2}}(\psi_{1}\psi_{2}+\psi_{1}\psi_{3}+\psi_{2}\psi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex113"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex113.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}(\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{13}+\Delta_{23})-\frac{3}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{1}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2}+\psi_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{2}}\kappa_{1}(\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{13}+\Delta_{23})-\frac{3}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{1}(\psi_{1}+\psi_{2}+\psi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex114"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex114.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{2}+\frac{3}{(2g-2)^{4}}\kappa_{1}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{3}}\kappa_{2}+\frac{3}{(2g-2)^{4}}\kappa_{1}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We call this the <em id="S6.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gross–Schoen cycle</em>. Being <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant, it defines a class in the summand <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\Sym^{3}\mathbf{V})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\Sym^{3}\mathbf{V})\subset\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes 3})</annotation></semantics></math>. There is now a decomposition</p>
<table id="S6.Ex115" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex115.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sym^{3}\mathbf{V}\cong\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}\oplus\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sym^{3}\mathbf{V}\cong\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}\oplus\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and one could try to define a “refined” Gross–Schoen cycle by projecting onto the summand <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>, just as we did for the Faber–Pandharipande cycle in the previous example. However, the difference between the refined and unrefined Gross–Schoen cycles would then be an element of <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L})=\mathrm{CH}^{1}(M_{g},\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>, which always vanishes. By using Nazarov’s theorem one can construct a “refined projector” onto <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>; the image of <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{123}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{123}</annotation></semantics></math> under this refined projector agrees with the image under the naive projector <math id="S6.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a nontrivial consistency check.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The cycle originally considered by Gross and Schoen in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS95</a>]</cite> is related to ours as follows. Let <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth curve and <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}</annotation></semantics></math> a degree <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> zero-cycle on <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then they studied the cycle</p>
<table id="S6.Ex116" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex116.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{123}-\mathfrak{z}_{1}\Delta_{23}-\mathfrak{z}_{2}\Delta_{13}-\mathfrak{z}_{3}\Delta_{12}+\mathfrak{z}_{2}\mathfrak{z}_{3}+\mathfrak{z}_{1}\mathfrak{z}_{3}+\mathfrak{z}_{1}\mathfrak{z}_{2}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{3})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>13</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{123}-\mathfrak{z}_{1}\Delta_{23}-\mathfrak{z}_{2}\Delta_{13}-\mathfrak{z}_{3}\Delta_{12}+\mathfrak{z}_{2}\mathfrak{z}_{3}+\mathfrak{z}_{1}\mathfrak{z}_{3}+\mathfrak{z}_{1}\mathfrak{z}_{2}\in\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{3}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔷</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pullback of <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}</annotation></semantics></math> from the projection map onto the <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th factor. Considering our cycle in <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and removing terms which obviously vanish gives</p>
<table id="S6.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex117"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex117.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{123}-\frac{1}{2g-2}((\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{1}+(\Delta_{13}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{2}+(\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{13})\psi_{3})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>13</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>23</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>13</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{123}-\frac{1}{2g-2}((\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{1}+(\Delta_{13}+\Delta_{23})\psi_{2}+(\Delta_{12}+\Delta_{13})\psi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex118"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex118.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{2}{(2g-2)^{2}}(\psi_{1}\psi_{2}+\psi_{1}\psi_{3}+\psi_{2}\psi_{3})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{2}{(2g-2)^{2}}(\psi_{1}\psi_{2}+\psi_{1}\psi_{3}+\psi_{2}\psi_{3})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which does <em id="S6.SS4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> coincide with the usual Gross–Schoen cycle for <math id="S6.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{z}=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔷</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{z}=\frac{1}{2g-2}\psi</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the difference between the two cycles is given by a sum of Faber–Pandharipande cycles. In particular, our Gross–Schoen cycle and the usual one will have the same image under the Abel–Jacobi map, since the Faber–Pandharipande cycle is Abel–Jacobi trivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Gross–Schoen cycle defines a nontrivial class in <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Nonvanishing of the Gross–Schoen cycle is equivalent to nonvanishing of the <em id="S6.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ceresa cycle</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cer83</a>]</cite>, which is known to be nonzero in <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{2}(X^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a very general curve of genus <math id="S6.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fak96</a>]</cite> for this result in positive characteristic.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By a theorem of Looijenga <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo95</a>]</cite>, it is known that</p>
<table id="S7.Ex119" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex119.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and that the tautological ring vanishes above this degree. More precisely, Looijenga proved the vanishing and that this group is at most <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional, and Faber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fab97</a>]</cite> found an example of a nonzero tautological class in this degree. The top nonzero degree of the tautological ring is called the <em id="S7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">socle</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This isomorphism can be made explicit in the following way. We define a map</p>
<table id="S7.Ex120" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex120.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2}(M_{g})\to\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2}(M_{g})\to\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">by</p>
<table id="S7.Ex121" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex121.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\mapsto\int_{\overline{M}_{g}}\overline{\alpha}\cdot\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\mapsto\int_{\overline{M}_{g}}\overline{\alpha}\cdot\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the closure of an algebraic cycle representing the class <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th Chern class of the Hodge bundle. We recall that the Hodge bundle is the locally free sheaf of rank <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber at a moduli point <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C]</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of holomorphic differentials on <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. A priori the integral would seem to not be well defined, since it depends on the choice of an algebraic cycle representing <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, but the integral is in fact well defined since multiplication by <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> kills everything supported on the Deligne–Mumford boundary. For <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, one has an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S7.Ex122" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex122.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\to R^{g-2}(M_{g})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2+n}(C_{g}^{n})\to R^{g-2}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.4" class="ltx_p">given by pushforward (with inverse given by pullback and multiplication by <math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{n}}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}\cdots\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{(2g-2)^{n}}\psi_{1}\psi_{2}\cdots\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All in all, this means that we have a pairing, which we denote by brackets:</p>
<table id="S7.Ex123" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.Ex123.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="49.8" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-24.9px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 262.46 49.8" width="262.46"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,49.8) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(131.23,0) translate(0,24.9)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -131.23 -16.38)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 49.8)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 16.81)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(84.95,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -79 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.71" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="144.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex123.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 180.73 0) translate(52.06,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -12.9 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.18" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="11.95"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex123.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 41.27)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 33.63 0) translate(51.33,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -45.37 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="76.9"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex123.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha\otimes\beta\hskip 31.2982pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:3.13em;" width="3.13em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\alpha\otimes\beta\hskip 31.2982pt}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 169.91 0) translate(62.88,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -23.71 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="33.59"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex123.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 38.95 11.55 L 81.87 11.55"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 82.15 11.55)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 5.74 -12.92 L 71.06 -12.92"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 5.6 -12.92)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 2.27 L 0 -2.27"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 71.33 -12.92)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">given by cup product, pushing down to <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and integrating against <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Arbitrary integrals of tautological classes on <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated algorithmically and efficiently <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fab99a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joh</a>]</cite>, in particular integrals over <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of top degree classes in <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> paired with <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g}\lambda_{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S7.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},\ldots,i_{n}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an equality</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex124" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex124.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\langle(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha,\beta\,\big\rangle=\big\langle\alpha,(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})\circ\beta\,\big\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\langle(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha,\beta\,\big\rangle=\big\langle\alpha,(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})\circ\beta\,\big\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> as a morphism <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\to h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as a morphism <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g}))\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-k}\to\mathbb{L}^{2-g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g}))\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-k}\to\mathbb{L}^{2-g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The composition <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\circ\alpha\in\mathsf{Mot}_{M_{g}}(\mathbf{1},\mathbb{L}^{2-g})=\mathrm{CH}^{g-2}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖬𝗈𝗍</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\circ\alpha\in\mathsf{Mot}_{M_{g}}(\mathbf{1},\mathbb{L}^{2-g})=\mathrm{CH}^{g-2}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is the product <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cdot\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cdot\beta</annotation></semantics></math> pushed forward to <math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now if <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is any correspondence <math id="S7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}\vdash C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>⊢</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}\vdash C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex125" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex125.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\circ(\pi\circ\alpha)=(\beta\circ\pi)\circ\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\circ(\pi\circ\alpha)=(\beta\circ\pi)\circ\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">But <math id="S7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\circ\pi=\pi^{t}\circ\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\circ\pi=\pi^{t}\circ\beta</annotation></semantics></math> by Remark <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Remark 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Chow groups and cohomology groups of a Chow motive ‣ 2. Chow motives ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Since <math id="S7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})^{t}=(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})^{t}=(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})</annotation></semantics></math> we are done.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in Subsection <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS3" title="5.2.3. Diagonals ‣ 5.2. Tautological maps ‣ 5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2.3</span></a>, the cup product in <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> (and then also in <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>) is in general not so easily described in terms of the relative Künneth decomposition of these algebras. The next proposition shows, however, that the <em id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">socle pairing</em> on <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> takes a very simple form with respect to the Künneth decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Gram matrix describing the socle pairing in the algebra <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is block-diagonal with respect to the relative Künneth decomposition of <math id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, the summand</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex126" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex126.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},h^{i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\subset R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},h^{i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\subset R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pairs to zero with all summands in complementary degree except for</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex127" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex127.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2+n-k}(M_{g},h^{2-i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{2-i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\subset R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2+n-k}(M_{g},h^{2-i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{2-i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))\subset R^{g-2+n-k}(C_{g}^{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in R^{k}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose it lies in the summand</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex128" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex128.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},h^{i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}));" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},h^{i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}));</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">equivalently, <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in complementary degree we have</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex129" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex129.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=\big\langle(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha,\beta\,\big\rangle=\big\langle\alpha,(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})\circ\beta\,\big\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=\big\langle(\pi_{i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{i_{n}})\circ\alpha,\beta\,\big\rangle=\big\langle\alpha,(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})\circ\beta\,\big\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">by the previous lemma. But if <math id="S7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> lies in any summand except for</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex130" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex130.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{g-2+n-k}(M_{g},h^{2-i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{2-i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{g-2+n-k}(M_{g},h^{2-i_{1}}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes\ldots\otimes h^{2-i_{n}}(C_{g}/M_{g})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">then <math id="S7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})\circ\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{2-i_{1}}\times\ldots\times\pi_{2-i_{n}})\circ\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle=0</annotation></semantics></math>, as claimed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous proposition shows that the socle pairing in <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the cup product maps <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{2-i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\otimes h^{2-i}(C_{g}/M_{g})\to h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})\cong\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since for <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2</annotation></semantics></math> these maps are given by the canonical isomorphisms <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbb{L}\to\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbb{L}\to\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{L}\otimes\mathbf{1}\to\mathbb{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕃</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{L}\otimes\mathbf{1}\to\mathbb{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, the socle pairing in fact only depends nontrivially on the maps</p>
<table id="S7.Ex131" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex131.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m})\otimes R^{g-2+m-k}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m})\to R^{g-2+m}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}^{\otimes m})\cong R^{g-2}(M_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m})\otimes R^{g-2+m-k}(M_{g},h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes m})\to R^{g-2+m}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}^{\otimes m})\cong R^{g-2}(M_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the level of Betti realizations, Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 7.2. ‣ 7. Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> says that even though the algebras <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}H^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> (and then also <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>) have very different cup product, both algebras <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> will have identical socle pairings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the algebra <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein (i.e. satisfies Poincaré duality) if and only if the same holds for the algebra <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. That said, Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 7.2. ‣ 7. Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> is not actually needed to prove this last fact. Since the Leray filtration is compatible with cup product, we know a priori that the Gram matrix describing the socle pairing for the algebra <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is block-triangular, and that the diagonal blocks coincide with the Gram matrix for the socle pairing in the algebra <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{gr}_{L}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular both matrices have the same rank. In these terms, Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 7.2. ‣ 7. Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> says that even though the intersection pairing for <math id="S7.Thmthm4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a priori only block-triangular, it turns out to actually be block-diagonal.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a genus <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All algebras </span><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are Gorenstein.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each partition </span><math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the pairing</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex132" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex132.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2+|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|})\cong R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2+|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|})\cong R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is perfect.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The pairing in (2) comes from the map of motives <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\to\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\to\mathbb{L}^{|\lambda|}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the fact that <math id="S7.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> is self-dual.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">If we decompose <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{n}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> as a direct sum of motives <math id="S7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the socle pairing in <math id="S7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of the various pairings</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex133" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex133.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus the socle pairing in the tautological ring of <math id="S7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect if and only if the same holds for the pairing for each of the motives <math id="S7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A variant of the preceding theorem, with the same proof, is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a genus <math id="S7.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All algebras </span><math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n\leq N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are Gorenstein.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each partition </span><math id="S7.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S7.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the pairing</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex134" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex134.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to R^{g-2}(M_{g})\cong\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is perfect.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We also wish to mention the following result, which was proven by somewhat different arguments in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pet16a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tav18a</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following statements are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All algebras </span><math id="S7.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S7.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq n\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq n\leq N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are Gorenstein.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The algebra </span><math id="S7.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g,N}^{\mathsf{rt}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g,N}^{\mathsf{rt}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is Gorenstein.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p">This shows that the Faber conjecture for the spaces <math id="S7.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math> can also be equivalently reformulated in terms of the motives <math id="S7.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. We saw in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5. Curves with rational tails ‣ 5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> that the tautological groups of <math id="S7.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed in terms of the tautological groups of the motives <math id="S7.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>; however, there does not appear to be an analogue of Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 7.2. ‣ 7. Consequences for Gorenstein conjectures ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> for the spaces <math id="S7.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math>: the socle pairing for <math id="S7.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>𝗋𝗍</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,n}^{\mathsf{rt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is block upper triangular with respect to the natural decomposition, but not in general block diagonal.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Symmetric powers</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We may also consider the <em id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">symmetric powers</em> of the universal curve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.8</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th symmetric power of the universal curve over <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{(n)}=C_{g}^{n}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{(n)}=C_{g}^{n}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define its tautological ring by <math id="S7.Thmthm8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})=R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})^{\mathfrak{S}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})=R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})^{\mathfrak{S}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that the Chow groups <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are decomposed as a direct sum of summands <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. The only local systems occuring in the subspace <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})\subseteq\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})\subseteq\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are those of the form <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(1,1,1,\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(1,1,1,\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. those that occur as summands of the symmetric powers of <math id="S7.Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider first the summand <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariants in this subspace are <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\Sym^{n}\mathbf{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\Sym^{n}\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves the lemma in this case. In general for <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=n_{0}+n_{1}+n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=n_{0}+n_{1}+n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the summand</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex135" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex135.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{0}}\otimes h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{1}}\otimes h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{2}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},h^{0}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{0}}\otimes h^{1}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{1}}\otimes h^{2}(C_{g}/M_{g})^{\otimes n_{2}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">together with its conjugates under the action of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, can be written as the induced representation</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex136" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex136.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathfrak{S}_{n_{0}}\times\mathfrak{S}_{n_{1}}\times\mathfrak{S}_{n_{2}}}^{\mathfrak{S}_{n}}\,\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n_{1}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{n_{2}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Ind</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝕃</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Ind}_{\mathfrak{S}_{n_{0}}\times\mathfrak{S}_{n_{1}}\times\mathfrak{S}_{n_{2}}}^{\mathfrak{S}_{n}}\,\mathrm{CH}^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n_{1}}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{n_{2}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, the <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariants in this induced representation are isomorphic to</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex137" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex137.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k-n_{2}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n_{1}})^{\mathfrak{S}_{n_{1}}}=\mathrm{CH}^{k-n_{2}}(M_{g},\Sym^{n_{1}}\mathbf{V})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k-n_{2}}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n_{1}})^{\mathfrak{S}_{n_{1}}}=\mathrm{CH}^{k-n_{2}}(M_{g},\Sym^{n_{1}}\mathbf{V})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">by Frobenius reciprocity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.10</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a genus <math id="S7.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S7.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I4.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a Gorenstein ring.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For some </span><math id="S7.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I4.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a Gorenstein ring.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The ring
<math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein if and only if all motives <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> occuring in the decomposition of <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C^{(n)}_{g}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C^{(n)}_{g}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> have the property that the pairing</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex138" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex138.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes R^{g-2+|\lambda|-k}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">is perfect. But the motives <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> occuring in the decomposition of the <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th symmetric power are exactly those with <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=(1,1,\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=(1,1,\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, by the previous lemma. The result follows from the fact that the motive <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1,\ldots\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle 1,1,1,\ldots\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero if the number of <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s is greater than <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, we can consider the “partial symmetric powers”, i.e. the tautological rings of <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-fold symmetric power of the universal curve over <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> these rings were considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin16</a>]</cite>, where they were proven to be intimately related to the tautological ring of the universal jacobian variety over <math id="S7.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}=M_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}=M_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The previous theorem admits a variant for the partial symmetric powers as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.11</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a genus <math id="S7.Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The following are equivalent:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S7.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I5.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I5.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a Gorenstein ring.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For some </span><math id="S7.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq g+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq g+k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a Gorenstein ring.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The relative Chow motive of <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n+k}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tensor product <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{(n)}/M_{g})\otimes h(C_{g}^{k}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{(n)}/M_{g})\otimes h(C_{g}^{k}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{k}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{k}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> only contains motives <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(C_{g}^{(n)}/M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(C_{g}^{(n)}/M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> only contains motives <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|\leq g</annotation></semantics></math> (by the argument of the preceding proof), the result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmthm12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.12</span></span><span id="S7.Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin16</a>, Theorem 7.15]</cite> it is proven that if <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+1}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+1}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein for some <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2g-1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+1}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n+1}/\mathfrak{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is Gorenstein for all <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof uses the relationship with the tautological ring of the universal jacobian <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n+1}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n+1}/\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a projective bundle over <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.Thmthm12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2g-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the arguments here re-prove this result with a slightly better lower bound.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Twisted commutative algebras and tautological rings</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the next sections we will analyze the structure of the collection of tautological rings <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> but varying <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. When we consider the direct sum <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{n}R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{n}R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain the structure of a <em id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted commutative algebra</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted associative algebra</em> is an <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{N}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded unital associative algebra (say over <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S8.Ex139" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex139.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}A(n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}A(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">together with an action of the symmetric group <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on the summand <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the multiplication</p>
<table id="S8.Ex140" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex140.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(n)\otimes A(m)\to A(n+m)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(n)\otimes A(m)\to A(n+m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is equivariant for the action of <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}\times\mathfrak{S}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}\times\mathfrak{S}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> on both sides. We say that <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">arity <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> component</em> of <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}A(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}A(n)</annotation></semantics></math> be a twisted associative algebra. We say that <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted commutative algebra</em> if the diagram</p>
<table id="S8.Ex141" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S8.Ex141.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="72.84" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-36.42px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 222.87 72.84" width="222.87"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,72.84) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(111.43,0) translate(0,36.42)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -111.43 -27.9)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 72.83)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 15.44)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(52.7,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -46.74 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="79.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex141.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(n)\otimes A(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(n)\otimes A(m)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 105.4 0) translate(75.34,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.17 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="58.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex141.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(n+m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(n+m)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 64.31)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(52.7,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -46.74 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.76em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="79.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex141.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(m)\otimes A(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(m)\otimes A(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 105.4 0) translate(75.34,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.17 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.23em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="58.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex141.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(m+n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces A(m+n)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -5.76 24.44 L 26.34 24.44"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 26.62 24.44)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -58.73 12.18 L -58.73 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 -58.73 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 69.3 12.18 L 69.3 -11.62"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(0.0 -1.0 1.0 0.0 69.3 -11.9)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.39998pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -5.76 -24.44 L 26.34 -24.44"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 26.62 -24.44)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.88 3.32 C -2.35 1.33 -1.18 0.39 0 0 C -1.18 -0.39 -2.35 -1.33 -2.88 -3.32"></path></g></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">commutes for all <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, where the horizontal maps are given by multiplication, the left vertical map swaps the two factors, and the right map is given by acting via the “box permutation” swapping the first <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and the last <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> elements.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 8.3</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us make three remarks concerning the definition.</p>
<ol id="S8.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In all our examples, we will have what should more properly be called a “twisted commutative graded algebra” — each summand <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is itself <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-graded, and the multiplication respects the <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-grading.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The notion of a twisted commutative <em id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graded</em> algebra is potentially ambiguous: in the commutativity condition, should the Koszul sign rule be applied to the map <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(n)\otimes A(m)\to A(m)\otimes A(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(n)\otimes A(m)\to A(m)\otimes A(n)</annotation></semantics></math> that swaps the two factors? In fact we will require both possible conventions in this paper: when working with Chow groups we do not impose the Koszul sign rule, but when working with cohomology groups we do impose it. This is because the Chow ring <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of an algebraic variety <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative in the strict sense, whereas the cohomology ring <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is commutative in the graded sense. We will pass over this ambiguity in silence for the rest of the paper; this should not cause any confusion.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are many equivalent ways to axiomatize the notion of a twisted commutative algebra. Here is an alternative one: let <math id="S8.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be the symmetric monoidal category of finite sets and bijections, with monoidal structure given by disjoint union. A twisted commutative algebra is a lax symmetric monoidal functor <math id="S8.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\to\mathsf{Vect}_{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖵𝖾𝖼𝗍</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\to\mathsf{Vect}_{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (or to the category of graded <math id="S8.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces, with or without the Koszul sign rule).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For more on twisted commutative algebras see e.g.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS10</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joy86</a>, Chapitre 4]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our main example will be the following. Fix a genus <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The direct sum <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an example of a twisted commutative algebra. The multiplication</p>
<table id="S8.Ex142" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex142.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes\mathrm{CH}^{l}(C_{g}^{m})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k+l}(C_{g}^{n+m})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{n})\otimes\mathrm{CH}^{l}(C_{g}^{m})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k+l}(C_{g}^{n+m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is given by the cross product, as defined in <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS1" title="5.2.1. Cross product ‣ 5.2. Tautological maps ‣ 5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2.1</span></a>. More generally, for any partition <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}=T\sqcup T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊔</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}=T\sqcup T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we have maps <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{T})\otimes\mathrm{CH}^{l}(C_{g}^{T^{\prime}})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k+l}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>CH</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CH}^{k}(C_{g}^{T})\otimes\mathrm{CH}^{l}(C_{g}^{T^{\prime}})\to\mathrm{CH}^{k+l}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We will refer to maps of this form, too, as cross product maps; this should not cause any confusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now have the following proposition, which in a sense explains why we will find the notion of a twisted commutative algebra useful. We have defined maps <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})\to R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})\to R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})\to R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})\to R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>; recall that <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the “primitive part” of <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math> and was defined in Subsection <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Decomposition into representations of the symplectic group ‣ 5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. These maps are not in any sense ring homomorphisms (in fact there is no ring structure on the latter two spaces). Nevertheless these will define homomorphisms of <em id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted</em> commutative algebras, when we consider all <math id="S8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously:</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.4</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S8.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the following commutative diagram:</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex143" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="137.68" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-68.84px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 561.59 137.68" width="561.59"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,137.68) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(280.8,0) translate(0,68.84)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -280.79 -49.94)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 137.68)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 20.46)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 80.31 0) translate(76.68,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -53.74 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.72em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="24.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="92.98"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 216.69 0) translate(94.18,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -71.25 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.25em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="24.85" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="127.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 388.09 0) translate(95.24,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -72.31 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.4em;--ltx-fo-height:1.11em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="29.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 15.4)" width="130.1"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>CH</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\mathrm{CH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\langle n\rangle})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 66.68)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(40.16,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.2 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.89em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="23.37" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="53.89"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}S_{n}^{\bullet}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}S_{n}^{\bullet}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 118.78)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 85.19 0) translate(71.8,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -48.87 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.01em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="24.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="83.22"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}R^{\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 221.57 0) translate(89.3,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -66.37 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.54em;--ltx-fo-height:0.8em;--ltx-fo-depth:1em;font-size:10pt;" height="24.85" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.02)" width="118.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}R^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{V}^{\otimes n})}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" s