</table>
<p id="S10.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> the Faber conjecture predicts the vanishing results <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1234}\Delta_{5678}\equiv\Delta_{123}\Delta_{456}\Delta_{78}\psi_{7}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1234</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>5678</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>456</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>78</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1234}\Delta_{5678}\equiv\Delta_{123}\Delta_{456}\Delta_{78}\psi_{7}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Assuming that the FZ relations are all relations between tautological classes, one finds that <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1234}\Delta_{5678}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1234</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>5678</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1234}\Delta_{5678}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{123}\Delta_{456}\Delta_{78}\psi_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>456</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>78</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{123}\Delta_{456}\Delta_{78}\psi_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> should both have nonzero image in <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{5}^{\prime\prime}(8)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{5}^{\prime\prime}(8)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we expect that</p>
<table id="S10.Ex188" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex188.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{6}(M_{5},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2,2,2,2\rangle})\cong R^{6}(M_{5},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 3,2,2,1\rangle})\cong\mathbf{Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{6}(M_{5},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 2,2,2,2\rangle})\cong R^{6}(M_{5},\mathbf{V}_{\langle 3,2,2,1\rangle})\cong\mathbf{Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Either of these nonvanishings would imply that <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(C_{5}^{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn><mn>8</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(C_{5}^{8})</annotation></semantics></math> is not Gorenstein. Proving them seems like a hard problem; nevertheless, we consider this to be progress in trying to find a counterexample to the Faber conjecture. Trying to prove that a specific cohomology group (or cohomology class) does not vanish is a far more appealing problem than, say, trying to prove that the rank of <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{6}(C_{5}^{8})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn><mn>8</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{6}(C_{5}^{8})</annotation></semantics></math> is greater than <math id="S10.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35166" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35166</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35166</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, our approach relates the Faber conjecture to actively studied questions about <em id="S10.SS4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">modified diagonals</em>, see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">O’G14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi15</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">MY16</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11. </span>Relation to work of Looijenga</h2>

<section id="S11.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11.1. </span>The theorems of Harer and Madsen–Weiss</h3>

<div id="S11.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,\vec{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,\vec{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the moduli space parametrizing smooth genus <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves equipped with a marked point and a nonzero tangent vector at the marking. The (analytifications of the) spaces <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,\vec{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,\vec{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> are both <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\pi,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\pi,1)</annotation></semantics></math> spaces in the orbifold sense, meaning in particular that their cohomology is given by the group cohomology of their fundamental groups. Whereas the (orbifold) fundamental group of <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapping class group of a closed genus <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface, the fundamental group of <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,\vec{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,\vec{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapping class group of a genus <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface with a parametrized boundary component. As such, there is a “stabilization” map</p>
<table id="S11.Ex189" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex189.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(M_{g,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})\to H_{k}(M_{g+1,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(M_{g,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})\to H_{k}(M_{g+1,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which on the level of fundamental groups is given by gluing a torus with two boundary components onto the boundary of the genus <math id="S11.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface. See also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL97</a>, Section 4]</cite> for how to define these stabilization maps algebro-geometrically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The celebrated stability theorem of Harer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har85</a>]</cite> asserts that the stabilization map is an isomorphism for <math id="S11.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.1</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S11.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(M_{g,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})\to H_{k}(M_{g+1,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(M_{g,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})\to H_{k}(M_{g+1,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an isomorphism for <math id="S11.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\frac{2}{3}(g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\frac{2}{3}(g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If we are interested primarily in closed surfaces, we also have a stabilization result for the forgetful map <math id="S11.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g,\vec{1}}\to M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g,\vec{1}}\to M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that forgets the marking and the tangent vector:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.2</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Harer)<span id="S11.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map <math id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(M_{g,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})\to H_{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(M_{g,\vec{1}},\mathbf{Z})\to H_{k}(M_{g},\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism for <math id="S11.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\frac{2}{3}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\frac{2}{3}g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The original bounds for the stable range of Harer have successively been improved by multiple people to obtain these results, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bol12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wah13</a>]</cite>. We note that to obtain stability with integer coefficients in Theorem <a href="#S11.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 11.2 (Harer). ‣ 11.1. The theorems of Harer and Madsen–Weiss ‣ 11. Relation to work of Looijenga ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.2</span></a> it is crucial that <math id="S11.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is considered as a stack — if we work with its coarse moduli space, the result is only valid with <math id="S11.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is a formidable problem to actually compute the stable homology of <math id="S11.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S11.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients, an answer was conjectured by Mumford and proven by Madsen–Weiss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">MW07</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.3</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Madsen–Weiss)<span id="S11.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map <math id="S11.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\kappa_{3},\ldots]\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\kappa_{3},\ldots]\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism in the stable range, i.e. in degrees up to <math id="S11.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2}{3}(g-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2}{3}(g-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 11.4</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we formally denote the value of the stable cohomology by <math id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the statement is that <math id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial algebra in the <math id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> classes. Since the tautological ring of <math id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the algebra generated by the <math id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> classes, it therefore makes sense say that <em id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the tautological cohomology of <math id="S11.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of the stable cohomology in the unstable cohomology.</em>
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">11.2. </span>Twisted coefficients</h3>

<div id="S11.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can also ask whether homological stability holds with coefficients in a local system <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, stabilization should be interpreted as appending an integer <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{g+1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{g+1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> to the weight vector <math id="S11.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\ldots\geq\lambda_{g}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\ldots\geq\lambda_{g}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The analogue of Harer stability holds in this case, too, by a theorem of Ivanov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iva93</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.5</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ivanov)<span id="S11.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map <math id="S11.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to H^{k}(M_{g+1},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\to H^{k}(M_{g+1},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism for <math id="S11.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg k,|\lambda|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg k,|\lambda|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We should remark that Ivanov’s statement was not specifically about the local systems <math id="S11.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>; his theorem is valid for a more general notion of coefficient system of finite degree which makes sense over an arbitrary base ring, and the local systems <math id="S11.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> are an example of such.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Ivanov’s theorem did not actually calculate the stable cohomology with twisted coefficients. Rationally, the stable cohomology with coefficients in <math id="S11.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> was calculated by Looijenga <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo96</a>]</cite>, in a paper that strongly influenced our way of thinking on these subjects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The first step in Looijenga’s calculation of <math id="S11.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> is to compute the stable cohomology of the spaces <math id="S11.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. His result can be reformulated in a rather appealing way in terms of twisted commutative algebras:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.6</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Looijenga)<span id="S11.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The twisted commutative algebra <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}H^{\bullet}(C_{\infty}^{n},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}H^{\bullet}(C_{\infty}^{n},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is free on <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant generators <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n,r}</annotation></semantics></math> in arity <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomological degree <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1,r\geq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1,r\geq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the map <math id="S11.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{n}^{\bullet}\to H^{2\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}^{\bullet}\to H^{2\bullet}(C_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism in the stable range.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We note that this theorem contains in particular the Madsen–Weiss theorem, by restricting to the case <math id="S11.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> (in which case the generators <math id="S11.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0,r}</annotation></semantics></math> are kappa classes), even though Looijenga’s paper predates the Madsen–Weiss theorem. Thus Looijenga’s theorem was rather that the stable cohomology of <math id="S11.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with twisted coefficients is a free module over the stable cohomology with constant coefficients with explicitly given generators; plugging in the Madsen–Weiss theorem gives the above result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To compute <math id="S11.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S11.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 11.6 (Looijenga). ‣ 11.2. Twisted coefficients ‣ 11. Relation to work of Looijenga ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11.6</span></a>, one notes that there is a surjection of twisted commutative algebras <math id="S11.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}H^{\bullet}(C_{\infty}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\to\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}H^{\bullet-n}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}H^{\bullet}(C_{\infty}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\to\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\bigoplus_{|\lambda|=n}H^{\bullet-n}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose kernel is the ideal generated by the classes <math id="S11.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus one finds:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S11.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.7</span></span><span id="S11.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Looijenga)<span id="S11.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S11.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The twisted commutative algebra <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet-|\lambda|}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet-|\lambda|}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the free twisted commutative algebra on <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant generators <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n,r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n,r}</annotation></semantics></math> in arity <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomological degree <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1,r\geq\max(2,n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1,r\geq\max(2,n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By decomposing this free twisted commutative algebra into irreducible representations of <math id="S11.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, one finds a calculation of the stable cohomology <math id="S11.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{\infty},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S11.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Looijenga does not state his result in these terms: he defines a certain algebra <math id="S11.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> which he decomposes into irreducible representations of <math id="S11.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this algebra (tensored with the polynomial ring in the kappa classes) is the arity <math id="S11.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> component of the free twisted commutative algebra in the previous result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The conclusion is in any case the following. For constant coefficents, the stable cohomology of <math id="S11.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S11.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">free polynomial algebra</em> on the <math id="S11.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-classes. The image of the stable cohomology inside the unstable cohomology can be defined to be the <em id="S11.SS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological cohomology of <math id="S11.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></em>. If we consider instead the stable cohomology with all possible twisted coefficients, i.e. the direct sum <math id="S11.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet-|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet-|\lambda|}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda^{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then this is a <em id="S11.SS2.p8.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">free twisted commutative algebra</em>, and the image of the stable cohomology inside the unstable cohomology is now exactly what we defined to be the <em id="S11.SS2.p8.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tautological cohomology of <math id="S11.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with twisted coefficients</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S12" class="ltx_section" lang="en">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12. </span>The “primary approximation” to the cohomology of the moduli space</h2>

<div id="S12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior to this paper, Hain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai13</a>]</cite> proposed a definition of tautological cohomology groups <math id="S12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with coefficients in a symplectic representation, which is a priori different from ours. In this section we will show that the two definitions coincide. In case <math id="S12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives a new proof of a theorem of Kawazumi and Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM96</a>]</cite>. We note that Hain asked in loc. cit. whether his construction could be lifted to the level of Chow groups; our constructions provide such a lifting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\Sp({2g}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\Sp({2g}))</annotation></semantics></math> be the algebraic coordinate ring of the symplectic group over <math id="S12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the Peter–Weyl theorem, there is an isomorphism of <math id="S12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)\times\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)\times\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-bimodules</p>
<table id="S12.Ex190" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex190.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\Sp(2g))\cong\bigoplus_{\lambda}V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\Sp(2g))\cong\bigoplus_{\lambda}V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum runs over all irreducible representations of the symplectic group. We consider <math id="S12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> to have a left action and <math id="S12.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> with a right action. Using the left action of <math id="S12.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S12.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\Sp(2g))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\Sp(2g))</annotation></semantics></math>, we may consider it as defining a local system of algebras <math id="S12.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g))</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S12.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking its cohomology, we get that</p>
<table id="S12.Ex191" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex191.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}_{g}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{def}}}{{=}}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))=\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mover><mo>=</mo><mi>def</mi></mover><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}_{g}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{def}}}{{=}}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))=\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is in a natural way a commutative <math id="S12.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">HL97</a>, Section 9.5]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 12.1</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A perhaps more down to earth way to understand this multiplication is as follows. Suppose that we have <math id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\supset V_{\langle\nu\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\supset V_{\langle\nu\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we get a multiplication map</p>
<table id="S12.Ex192" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex192.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which however depends nontrivially on the choice of an intertwiner <math id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\to V_{\langle\nu\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\to V_{\langle\nu\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. What is instead completely well defined is the map</p>
<table id="S12.Ex193" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex193.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which (using the canonical identification <math id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(M,N)=N\otimes M^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(M,N)=N\otimes M^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>) can be thought of equivalently as an <math id="S12.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map</p>
<table id="S12.Ex194" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex194.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}^{\ast}\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\nu\rangle}^{\ast}.\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}^{\ast}\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\nu\rangle}^{\ast}.\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now consider the Gross–Schoen cycle as a class <math id="S12.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{1}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>. We have a vector subspace <math id="S12.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\otimes V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\mathsf{T}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\otimes V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\mathsf{T}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore by the universal property of a polynomial algebra a morphism of graded commutative rings</p>
<table id="S12.Ex195" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex195.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝖳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p3.2" class="ltx_p">There is an inclusion <math id="S12.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\wedge^{2}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\wedge^{2}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S12.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological, every class in the image of this homomorphism is tautological; it follows from this that the summand <math id="S12.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\wedge^{2}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\subset\wedge^{2}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the kernel, since one can compute from our results (or rather the work of Looijenga) that <math id="S12.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 2,2\rangle})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{2}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 2,2\rangle})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the algebra <math id="S12.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}/(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}/(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S12.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that there exist an <math id="S12.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant ring homomorphism</p>
<table id="S12.Ex196" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex196.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 12.2</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">H-tautological ring</em> is the subring <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}\subset\mathsf{T}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}\subset\mathsf{T}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> given as the image of <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. By decomposing the <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological ring into irreducible summands for its natural action of <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> we get a subspace of <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological classes inside <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> for any partition <math id="S12.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 12.3</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S12.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> the local system <math id="S12.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes (and so does the Gross–Schoen cycle), and the <math id="S12.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological ring consists only of the unit element in <math id="S12.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(M_{2},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 0\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(M_{2},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 0\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 12.4</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The definition above may seem very ad hoc — why should the Gross–Schoen cycle play a more distinguished role than any other tautological class? A more “invariant” definition is that the <math id="S12.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological ring is the subring of <math id="S12.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))</annotation></semantics></math> generated by all normal functions over <math id="S12.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai95</a>]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 12.5</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is a striking fact that unlike the usual tautological ring of <math id="S12.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S12.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S12.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological ring is generated by a single algebraic cycle class.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Restricting to symplectic invariants, we get a map</p>
<table id="S12.Ex197" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex197.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}=H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}\to\mathsf{T}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}=H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This morphism is exactly what Morita calls the <em id="S12.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">primary approximation</em> to the cohomology ring of <math id="S12.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Morita originally described it in rather different terms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor93</a>]</cite>; this re-interpretation is due to Hain. A theorem of Kawazumi and Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM96</a>]</cite> asserts that the image of <math id="S12.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological cohomology ring of <math id="S12.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will prove a more general result below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remarkx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 12.6</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above map can also be understood in terms of relative Malcev completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai97</a>]</cite>. Hain constructs a Lie algebra <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{u}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{u}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of mixed Hodge structures with <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-action (the Lie algebra of the pro-unipotent radical of the relative completion of the mapping class group) and an <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant map <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(\mathfrak{u}_{g})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(\mathfrak{u}_{g})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))</annotation></semantics></math>. The results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hai97</a>]</cite> (and subsequent improvements) show that the weights of <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{u}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{u}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are negative and that <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}^{W}_{-1}\mathfrak{u}_{g}\cong V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}^{W}_{-1}\mathfrak{u}_{g}\cong V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Gr}^{W}_{-2}\mathfrak{u}_{g}\cong V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Gr}^{W}_{-2}\mathfrak{u}_{g}\cong V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that the algebra <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pure part <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{k}W_{k}H^{k}(\mathfrak{u}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{k}W_{k}H^{k}(\mathfrak{u}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> of the Chevalley–Eilenberg cohomology of <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{u}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{u}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological ring can also be defined as the image of the lowest weight part of the cohomology of <math id="S12.Thmthm6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{u}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔲</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{u}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 12.7</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,m</annotation></semantics></math> be integers with <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2m-2&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2m-2&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Construct a <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\cdot(n+2m-2),n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\cdot(n+2m-2),n)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagram as follows: for <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,n+2m-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,n+2m-3</annotation></semantics></math>, draw a horizontal strand connecting the <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3i)</annotation></semantics></math>th node on the top row to the <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3i+1)</annotation></semantics></math>st. Of the remaining <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2m</annotation></semantics></math> nodes on the top row, pick <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of them arbitrarily and connect them to the nodes along the bottom row, and connect the remaining <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math> nodes arbitrarily to each other by <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> horizontal strands. Consider the resulting map</span></p>
<table id="S12.Ex198" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex198.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{n+2m-2}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes 3(n+2m-2)})\to H^{n+2m-2}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{n+2m-2}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes 3(n+2m-2)})\to H^{n+2m-2}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\otimes n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The image of <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times 3}(\Delta_{123})^{\times(n+2m-2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times 3}(\Delta_{123})^{\times(n+2m-2)}</annotation></semantics></math> under this map is the class <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times n}(\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times n}(\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S12.Thmthm7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is an easy consequence of the discussion in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Decomposition into representations of the symplectic group ‣ 5. The Künneth decomposition of the tautological ring ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. Namely, to compute the image of <math id="S12.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times 3}(\Delta_{123})^{\times(n-2+2m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times 3}(\Delta_{123})^{\times(n-2+2m)}</annotation></semantics></math> we start with the cycle <math id="S12.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{123}\Delta_{456}\Delta_{789}\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>123</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>456</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>789</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{123}\Delta_{456}\Delta_{789}\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>, restrict to a suitable diagonal locus — specifically, the diagonals are specified by the horizontal strands in the Brauer diagram — and then project away from the markings corresponding to these diagonals. This gives <math id="S12.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S12.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S12.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S12.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, after which we should apply <math id="S12.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}^{\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}^{\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives the result.∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12.8</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The space of <math id="S12.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological classes inside <math id="S12.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the space <math id="S12.Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))=\bigoplus_{\lambda}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\lambda}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))=\bigoplus_{\lambda}{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\lambda}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> of tautological classes in our sense.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We note first that <math id="S12.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))</annotation></semantics></math> is a subalgebra of <math id="S12.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g))</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, consider the multiplication map</span></p>
<table id="S12.Ex199" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex199.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Every element of <math id="S12.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by Brauer diagrams. It follows that if we realize the cohomologies of the different local systems as summands of the cohomologies of fibered powers <math id="S12.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the multiplication <math id="S12.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle})\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by an algebraic correspondence given by tautological cycles,, for any choice of intertwiner <math id="S12.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\to V_{\langle\nu\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle}\to V_{\langle\nu\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means in particular that the cup product maps tautological classes to tautological classes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, this means that every <math id="S12.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological class is a tautological class in our sense: since the <math id="S12.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-tautological ring is generated by the Gross–Schoen cycle, it must be contained in the subalgebra of all tautological classes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely we need to prove that every tautological class in ours sense can be written as a product of Gross–Schoen cycles. It is enough to prove this for the generators of the twisted commutative algebra <math id="S12.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the images of the classes <math id="S12.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S12.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-classes. If we consider the Brauer diagram of Lemma <a href="#S12.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 12.7. ‣ 12. The “primary approximation” to the cohomology of the moduli space ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.7</span></a> as an element of <math id="S12.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}((\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})^{\otimes(n-2+2m)},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}((\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})^{\otimes(n-2+2m)},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle}),</annotation></semantics></math> then the image of this Brauer diagram and <math id="S12.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2+2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2+2m</annotation></semantics></math> copies of the Gross–Schoen cycle under the cup product map</span></p>
<table id="S12.Ex200" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex200.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}((\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})^{\otimes(n-2+2m)},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})\otimes H^{1}(M_{g},(\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})^{\otimes(n-2+2m)}\to H^{n-2+2m}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}((\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})^{\otimes(n-2+2m)},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})\otimes H^{1}(M_{g},(\mathbb{V}_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle})^{\otimes(n-2+2m)}\to H^{n-2+2m}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">equals the image of <math id="S12.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{12\ldots n}\psi_{1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> under the projection <math id="S12.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2(n-1+m)}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\to H^{n-2+2m}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2(n-1+m)}(C_{g}^{n},\mathbf{Q})\to H^{n-2+2m}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math>, as Lemma <a href="#S12.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 12.7. ‣ 12. The “primary approximation” to the cohomology of the moduli space ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.7</span></a> shows. The result follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 12.9</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kawazumi–Morita)<span id="S12.Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.Thmthm9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The image of <math id="S12.Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}/V_{\langle 2,2\rangle})^{\Sp(2g)}\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}/V_{\langle 2,2\rangle})^{\Sp(2g)}\to H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbf{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is the tautological cohomology ring of <math id="S12.Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is the case <math id="S12.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> of the preceding theorem.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Morita <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mor06</a>]</cite> has conjectured that the map <math id="S12.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective: that is, it defines an isomorphism between <math id="S12.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the tautological ring <math id="S12.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\bullet}(M_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\bullet}(M_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared to other conjectural descriptions of the tautological ring, e.g. the conjecture that all relations are consequences of the FZ relations, this gives a remarkably quick and direct description of the tautological ring (even though it is not so easy to determine the structure of the algebra <math id="S12.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math>). A natural generalization of Morita’s conjecture is to ask whether <math id="S12.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{R}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\colon\mathsf{A}_{g}\to\mathsf{R}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. A consequence of our results is that this is not the case, however:</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S12.Thmthm10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 12.10</span></span><span id="S12.Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S12.Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map <math id="S12.Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is not injective when <math id="S12.Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S12.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using a computer algebra system, one can verify that the third exterior power <math id="S12.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{3}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{3}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the irreducible representation <math id="S12.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle 3,2,2,2\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle 3,2,2,2\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> as a summand. On the other hand the degree <math id="S12.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> part of the ideal <math id="S12.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient of <math id="S12.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\otimes V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast}\otimes V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains only representations of weight at most <math id="S12.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S12.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> has a nontrivial summand <math id="S12.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle 3,2,2,2\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle 3,2,2,2\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S12.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. But our calculations of the tautological groups with twisted coefficients in genus four show that <math id="S12.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{3}(M_{4},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 3,2,2,2\rangle})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{R\hskip-0.5ptH}^{3}(M_{4},\mathbb{V}_{\langle 3,2,2,2\rangle})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so this summand must be in the kernel of <math id="S12.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p12.1" class="ltx_p">As pointed out in the introduction of this section, Hain asked whether the construction of a tautological algebra inside <math id="S12.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)))</annotation></semantics></math> could be lifted to the level of Chow groups, and our construction in this paper gives an affirmative answer to this question. However, there does not seem to be any sensible way to get the grading on the level of Chow groups, at least not without introducing fractional Tate twists. The source of the problem is that an intertwiner in <math id="S12.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes V_{\langle\mu\rangle},V_{\langle\nu\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> does not give rise to a morphism of Chow motives <math id="S12.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbf{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle}\to\mathbf{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbf{V}_{\langle\mu\rangle}\to\mathbf{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> unless <math id="S12.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|+|\mu|=|\nu|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|+|\mu|=|\nu|</annotation></semantics></math>; in general one only gets a morphism to a Tate twist of <math id="S12.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\nu\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>. One option is to work instead with Chow motives with respect to ungraded correspondences — one can make sense of <math id="S12.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g))</annotation></semantics></math> as a relative Chow motive over <math id="S12.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to ungraded correspondences — but the Chow groups of a motive with respect to ungraded correspondences only form a vector space, not a graded vector space, and so the grading needs to be put in “by hand”. Alternatively, if we allow half-integer Tate twists, then we can replace <math id="S12.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S12.p12.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-|\lambda|/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝕃</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\mathbb{L}^{-|\lambda|/2}</annotation></semantics></math> throughout, which will allow us to recover the cohomological grading (halved).</p>
</div>
<section id="S12.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12.1. </span>The twisted commutative algebra and the Peter–Weyl theorem</h3>

<div id="S12.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have now seen two a priori completely different ways to build an algebra by considering all local systems <math id="S12.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously: the commutative ring <math id="S12.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}_{g}=\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}_{g}=\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and the twisted commutative algebra <math id="S12.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{\lambda}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Remark <a href="#S8.Thmthm12" title="Remark 8.12. ‣ 8. Twisted commutative algebras and tautological rings ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.12</span></a>). We now want to explain a connection
between the two constructions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a ring in the category of graded <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-representations. We associate to <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> two twisted commutative algebras <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S12.Ex201" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex201.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\mapsto L_{A}(n)=(A\otimes V^{\otimes n})^{\Sp(2g)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mapsto L_{A}(n)=(A\otimes V^{\otimes n})^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S12.Ex202" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex202.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\mapsto L_{A}^{\circ}(n)=(A\otimes V^{\langle n\rangle})^{\Sp(2g)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mapsto L_{A}^{\circ}(n)=(A\otimes V^{\langle n\rangle})^{\Sp(2g)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">(Recall that <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\langle n\rangle}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\langle n\rangle}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}V_{\langle\lambda\rangle}\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.) The multiplication in <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S12.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S12.Ex203"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex203.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{A}(n)\otimes L_{A}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{A}(n)\otimes L_{A}(m)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex203.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(A\otimes V^{\otimes n})^{\Sp(2g)}\otimes(A\otimes V^{\otimes m})^{\Sp(2g)}\subseteq(A\otimes V^{\otimes n}\otimes A\otimes V^{\otimes m})^{\Sp(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(A\otimes V^{\otimes n})^{\Sp(2g)}\otimes(A\otimes V^{\otimes m})^{\Sp(2g)}\subseteq(A\otimes V^{\otimes n}\otimes A\otimes V^{\otimes m})^{\Sp(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S12.Ex204"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex204.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{mult.}}}{{\longrightarrow}}(A\otimes V^{\otimes n}\otimes V^{\otimes m})^{\Sp(2g)}=L_{A}(n+m)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>mult</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{mult.}}}{{\longrightarrow}}(A\otimes V^{\otimes n}\otimes V^{\otimes m})^{\Sp(2g)}=L_{A}(n+m),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">and similarly for <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We obtain two functors from the category of rings with action of <math id="S12.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math> to the category of twisted commutative algebras.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will apply our functors to the rings <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{A}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{A}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
            Strictly speaking these are rings in the category of representations of <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)^{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)^{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>: if we want to work with usual representations we should have <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}/(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}/(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="footnote2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}/(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mo>∙</mo></msup><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{\bullet}V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}/(V_{\langle 2,2\rangle}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math>. We will ignore this detail.</span></span></span> We find for example that</p>
<table id="S12.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S12.Ex205"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex205.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\mathsf{T}_{g}}^{\circ}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\mathsf{T}_{g}}^{\circ}(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex205.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{Hom}_{\Sp(2g)}(H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)),V^{\langle n\rangle})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{Hom}_{\Sp(2g)}(H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}(\Sp(2g)),V^{\langle n\rangle})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S12.Ex206"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex206.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong\bigoplus_{\lambda}\bigoplus_{\mu\vdash n}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes\mathrm{Hom}_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\lambda},V_{\mu})\otimes\sigma_{\mu}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong\bigoplus_{\lambda}\bigoplus_{\mu\vdash n}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\lambda})\otimes\mathrm{Hom}_{\Sp(2g)}(V_{\lambda},V_{\mu})\otimes\sigma_{\mu}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S12.Ex207"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex207.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}=H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong\bigoplus_{\lambda\vdash n}H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}_{\langle\lambda\rangle})\otimes\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast}=H^{\bullet}(M_{g},\mathbb{V}^{\langle n\rangle}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{R}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{R}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{R}_{g}}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{R}_{g}}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> are the cohomological analogues of the twisted commutative algebras denoted <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S8" title="8. Twisted commutative algebras and tautological rings ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. So our twisted commutative algebras can be recovered functorially from Hain’s tautological ring <math id="S12.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which explains how the two constructions are related.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A slightly more refined version of the above construction is possible. Recall that a twisted commutative algebra can be defined as a lax symmetric monoidal functor from <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coprod_{n\geq 0}\mathfrak{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coprod_{n\geq 0}\mathfrak{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to graded vector spaces. We define instead a <em id="S12.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">twisted Brauer algebra</em> as a lax symmetric monoidal functor from <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> to graded vector spaces. Recall that the category <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> was defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Brauer algebra ‣ 3. Preliminaries from representation theory ‣ Tautological classes with twisted coefficients" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>; it is the category whose objects are the natural numbers and morphisms <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to m</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagrams, with symmetric monoidal structure given on objects by addition and on morphisms by disjoint union. There is an evident inclusion <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coprod_{n\geq 0}\mathfrak{S}_{n}\to\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coprod_{n\geq 0}\mathfrak{S}_{n}\to\mathfrak{Br}^{(-2g)}</annotation></semantics></math>, by interpreting a bijection <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]\to[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]\to[n]</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S12.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagram with only vertical strands, which defines a forgetful functor from twisted Brauer algebras to twisted commutative algebras.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to see that if <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a ring with <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sp(2g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sp(2g)</annotation></semantics></math>-action, then <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> may in fact be considered as a twisted Brauer algebra.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
            One may also consider <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> as a twisted Brauer algebra, but one does not gain anything in doing so: all maps <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{A}^{\circ}(n)\to L_{A}^{\circ}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{A}^{\circ}(n)\to L_{A}^{\circ}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="footnote3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\neq m</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="footnote3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-Brauer diagrams are zero.</span></span></span> This has some advantages. For example, we noted that the ring <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{R}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{R}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a single algebraic cycle class (more precisely, by a single copy of the representation <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\langle 1,1,1\rangle}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas the twisted commutative tautological algebras <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{g}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{g}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> had large numbers of generators. If we consider <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathsf{R}_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>𝖱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathsf{R}_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a twisted <em id="S12.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Brauer</em> algebra rather than a twisted commutative algebra, it is in fact generated by a single element in arity <math id="S12.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the Gross–Schoen cycle. This shows that by considering twisted Brauer algebras one retains slightly more information.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
